n          \"2124-09-26 00:00:00\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"storetime\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"object\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 9997,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"2137-10-29 11:09:00\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"text\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 10000,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \" \\nName:  ___                   Unit No:   ___\\n \\nAdmission Date:  ___              Discharge Date:   ___\\n \\nDate of Birth:  ___             Sex:   M\\n \\nService: MEDICINE\\n \\nAllergies: \\nAmoxicillin\\n \\nAttending: ___.\\n \\nChief Complaint:\\nAbdominal pain/Shortness of breath \\n \\nMajor Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\\nTherapeutic Paracentesis\\n\\n \\nHistory of Present Illness:\\n___ y/o M w/ ___ history of metastatic melanoma ___ level \\nIV) with bony mets (spine and xiphoid) and ___ history of \\nmetastatic renal cell carcinoma (s/p 2 cycles of biochemotherapy \\non maintenance IL-2 and left nephrectomy with mets to the lung \\nand mediastinal lymph nodes) who was recently admited with new \\nonset of ascites causing abdominal pain, constipation, & nausea. \\nThat admission he had 2 paracentesis with 4L off (total). He was \\nseen in clinic today after he complained of right calf pain \\nyesterday and was sent to an OSH where US showed DVT, at which \\ntime he was started on Lovenox. At his visit today, he received \\ncycle 1 day 1 of Taxol, and was complaining of worsening dyspnea \\nand abdominal distention, so he was admitted for inpatient \\nparacentesis.  \\n\\n \\nOn arrival to the floor, he reports feeling \\\"good\\\". He reports \\nsubjective shortness of breath, which he feels as \\\"I can't get \\nenough air\\\" and he feels is directly related to his increased \\nabdominal distention. He denies pleuritic chest pain, and he \\nreports that his shortness of breath improves with sitting up \\nand worsens with lying flat. He denies headache, visual changes, \\nchest pain, palpitations, rash, dysuria, BRBPR, and dark tarry \\nstools. His abdominal pain is much improved from last admission, \\nand he had two episodes of nausea and vomiting today which \\nimproved with ativan.  \\n\\n \\nPast Medical History:\\nPast Oncologic History (per OMR):  \\nIn ___ a xiphoid lesion was excised and was consistent with  \\na 1.05 mm melanoma ___ level IV. After excision, there was no \\nevidence of residual melanocytic proliferation. No lymph node \\nsampling was performed. He was subsequently seen in close \\nfollow-up by Dr. ___.  \\n\\n \\nIn ___, he presented to the ___ ED with left flank \\npain at which time a CT scan showed a left renal mass. He \\nunderwent left radical nephrectomy in ___ at ___. \\nPathology revealed renal cell carcinoma, 10 cm, conventional \\nclear cell type grade ___. This was subsequently followed with \\nCT scan, which showed some small stable lung nodules for a \\nwhile, but in ___, the lung nodules appeared to be enlarging. \\nHe then developed some mediastinal lymphadenopathy and underwent \\nmediastinoscopy in ___ at ___ with an excised node \\nconsistent with metastatic renal cell carcinoma. He subsequently \\nreceived care at ___ beginning in ___ \\nwith Dr. ___ who recommended Sutent therapy. He was \\nstarted on Sutent 50 mg p.o. daily by his local oncologist, Dr. \\n___. Given that he had started on Sutent, he was not eligible \\nfor IL-2 at the time of his visit in ___. He continued to have \\nroutine imaging studies that showed response to Sutent, but in \\n___, a left axillary node was seen on imaging. He underwent a \\nfine-needle aspirate on ___ that was consistent with \\nmetastatic melanoma. He then underwent axillary dissection on \\n___, which confirmed melanoma. He had a PET scan right \\nbefore the wide excision on ___. This showed two soft \\ntissue densities in the left axilla, as well as increased \\nactivity in the T10 vertebral body that was not noted on \\nprevious examination and was suspicious for metastatic disease. \\nHe underwent MRI of his thoracic spine on ___, which \\nrevealed metastatic lesions in the vertebral bodies at T8, T5, \\nand a lytic lesion in T10. There were also small lytic lesions \\nat the superior endplate of L2. Given these findings, he \\nunderwent a vertebral bone biopsy, which was consistent with \\nmetastatic malignant melanoma. He continued to follow up with \\n___ at ___ and was then referred here for \\nfurther management of both renal cell carcinoma and metastatic \\nmelanoma. He underwent a repeat PET scan on ___, which \\nrevealed a wide spread avidity most intense in the left axilla, \\nas well as wide spread metastatic disease, heterogenous tracer \\nuptake in the liver with a focus most intense in the segment 6 \\nof the liver, focal uptake in the distal esophagus that was \\nnonspecific, and non-FDG avid pulmonary nodules that were too \\nsmall to characterize on PET CT. He underwent a MRI of the spine \\non ___, which revealed diffuse metastatic disease involving \\nC5, C6, and C7; T1, T5, T8, T10, and T11; L1 and L2; as well as \\ndisease in both the sacrum and both iliac ala. There was no \\nevidence of cord or spinal canal compression at the time. There \\nwas also at least one metastatic lesion noted at the right lung \\nbase.  \\n\\n \\nHe has received two cycles of biochemotherapy. His most recent \\ncycle was from ___ to ___. His first cycle was \\ncomplicated by neutropenia and significant nausea and vomiting \\nrequiring home IV fluids and IV antiemetics. His second cycle \\ninvolved reduced doses of chemotherapy agents but he still had \\nsevere N/V. PICC line was placed prior to discharge to allow for \\nhome IV fluids and antiemetics. He was recently admitted for \\nabdominal pain which improved with two therapeutic and \\ndiagnostic paracentesis.\\n.\\nPMH (per OMR):  \\n1) Metastatic melanoma  \\n2) Metastatic RCC  \\n3) Anemia - b/l Hct ~30%  \\n4) Mild HTN  \\n5) Hypercholesterolemia  \\n\\n \\nSocial History:\\n___\\nFamily History:\\nNo family history of melanoma or renal cell cancer. Father died \\nat ___ years of age from lung cancer. He also had prostate \\ncancer. His mother is still alive at ___ and is healthy. He has \\none sister who is healthy. A maternal aunt had breast cancer. He \\nhas one daughter that is healthy.  \\n\\n \\nPhysical Exam:\\nV/S: T 97.6F, BP 108/79, HR 96, RR 18, Sat 93%RA  \\nGEN: NAD, alert and oriented x 3  \\nHEENT: anicteric, PERLA, EOMI  \\nCV: RRR, no M/R/G  \\nPULM: Decreased breath sounds at the bases, otherwise clear  \\nABD: mild-moderate distention with palpable fluid wave, active \\nbowel sounds. No rebound/guarding.  \\nEXT: Right leg with 1+ edema > left leg, 2+ DP pulses \\nbilaterally; warm, well-perfused  \\nNEURO: CN II-XII intact. A&O x 3. Strength ___ in both upper and \\nlower extremities bilaterally. No sensory deficits.  \\n\\n \\nPertinent Results:\\nAdmission Labs:\\n___ 12:52PM   WBC-9.9 RBC-3.78* HGB-11.9* HCT-33.7* MCV-89 \\nMCH-31.5 MCHC-35.4* RDW-16.8*\\n___ 12:52PM   PLT COUNT-458*\\n___ 12:52PM   ___ ___\\n___ 12:52PM   ALBUMIN-3.3* CALCIUM-8.4 PHOSPHATE-4.2 \\nMAGNESIUM-1.9\\n___ 12:52PM   ALT(SGPT)-12 AST(SGOT)-20 LD(LDH)-1248* ALK \\nPHOS-85 TOT BILI-0.5 DIR BILI-0.1 INDIR BIL-0.4\\n___ 12:52PM   GLUCOSE-130* UREA N-18 CREAT-1.2 SODIUM-134 \\nPOTASSIUM-4.1 CHLORIDE-99 TOTAL CO2-25 ANION GAP-14\\n.\\nParacentesis:\\nIMPRESSION: Successful therapeutic paracentesis with 2.5 liters \\nof straw-\\ncolored ascitic fluid drained via the right lower quadrant.\\n\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\n___ metastatic melanoma and metastatic renal cell carcinoma with \\nascites found to have newly diagnosed peritoneal carcinomatosis \\n(melanoma), recent admission for therapeutic paracentesis, \\npresented with subjective shortness of breath, new DVT on \\nLovenox, and worsening abdominal distention.  \\n.\\n# Shortness of breath. Most likely secondary to restriction from \\nascitic fluid, although in setting of DVT, PE was considered. \\nHowever the patient was already anticoagulated and not requiring \\noxygen. Lung exam clear; no evidence of pneumonia (no cough, \\nfever, etc.) The patient proceeded with a therapeutic \\nparacentesis (2.5L removed) and that relieved his symtoms.\\n. \\n# Abdominal distention. Secondary to reaccumulating ascites, \\nalthough differential includes obstruction (chronic narcotics, \\nbut with + bowel sounds, recent benign imaging and recent bowel \\nmovement).  The patient was made NPO after midnight for \\ntherapeutic paracentesis, had his Lovenox held, placed on \\nheparin drip overnight, and stopped four hours prior to \\nprocedure. The patient had 2.5L that relieved his symtoms. \\n.\\n# Metastatic melanoma and renal cell carcinoma. The patient was \\ncontinued on a fentanyl patch, oxyconting and oxycodone PRN for \\npain control. \\n. \\n# DVT. Discovered one day prior to admission and was started on \\nLovenox. The patient had mild left sided reproducible calf pain. \\nThe patient was discharged on Lovenox. The patient stated he \\ndraws the equivalent of 85mg BID.\\n.\\n# Anemia. The pt presented with a hct slightly above baseline \\nwith a question of hemoconcentration. This was monitored without \\nintervention.  \\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\nLovenox 85mg BID  \\nFentanyl TD 75 mcg  \\nOxycontin 10mg BID  \\nOxycodone 5mg Q4-6H PRN (but not taking)  \\nAtivan 0.5 mg q4h/prn nausea  \\nCompazine 10 mg q6h/prn nausea  \\nTylenol prn  \\nColace prn  \\n\\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1. Docusate Sodium 100 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO BID (2 \\ntimes a day).  \\n2. Senna 8.6 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2 times a \\nday) as needed.  \\n3. Fentanyl 75 mcg/hr Patch 72 hr Sig: One (1) Patch 72 hr \\nTransdermal Q72H (every 72 hours).  \\n4. Oxycodone 10 mg Tablet Sustained Release 12 hr Sig: One (1) \\nTablet Sustained Release 12 hr PO Q12H (every 12 hours).  \\n5. Oxycodone 5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO Q4H (every 4 \\nhours) as needed.  \\n6. Lovenox 80 mg/0.8 mL Syringe Sig: One (1)  Subcutaneous twice \\na day: Patient draws up 85mg (Other Provider).  \\n\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nHome\\n \\nDischarge Diagnosis:\\nPrimary Diagnosis\\n- Metastatic Melanoma\\n- Ascites (Peritoneal Carcinomatosis)\\n- Metastatic Renal Cell Carcinoma\\n\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nGood. Patient ambulating, taking good PO, and at his mental and \\nphyiscal baseline. \\n\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nYou were admitted to the hospital with abdominal pain and \\nshortness of breath. You underwent a paracentesis and had 2.5 \\nliters removed. You previously had deep vein thrombosis and were \\nkept on both Lovenox and Heparin while as an inpatient.\\n.\\nPlease continue to take all of your medications. No changes have \\nbeen made to your regimen\\n.\\nPlease keep all of your appointments as listed below.\\n.\\nPlease return to hospital if you experience shortness of breath, \\nworsening abdominal pain, fevers, chills. \\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}"
