\nare mildly thickened. There is no mitral valve prolapse. \\nModerate (2+) mitral regurgitation is seen. [Due to acoustic \\nshadowing, the severity of mitral regurgitation may be \\nsignificantly UNDERestimated.] Severe [4+] tricuspid \\nregurgitation is seen. There is moderate pulmonary artery \\nsystolic hypertension. [In the setting of at least moderate to \\nsevere tricuspid regurgitation, the estimated pulmonary artery \\nsystolic pressure may be underestimated due to a very high right \\natrial pressure.] There is no pericardial effusion. \\n\\nIMPRESSION: Bilateral pleural effusions are present. Well seated \\n___ 3 TAVR in aortic position with normal gradients/leaflet \\nmotion and no paravalvular leak. Normal left ventricular wall \\nthiciness with normal cavity size, and moderate global \\nhypokinesis. Restrictive filling pattern. At least moderate \\nmitral rgurgitation. Severe tricuspid regurgitation. At least \\nmoderate pulmonary artery systolic hypertension. Evidence of \\npressure/volume overload. \\n\\nCompared with the prior study (images reviewed) of ___, \\nthe degree of mitral regurgitation appears slightly less. Other \\nfindings appear similar. \\n\\nCXR ___\\nNew, mild pulmonary edema with unchanged bilateral pleural \\neffusions,\\natelectasis, and cardiomegaly.\\n\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\nMs. ___ is a ___ female with history of HFrEF (EF \\n42%), AS s/p TAVR (___) complicated by ___ \\ncardiac arrest, CAD status post CABG, ESRD on HD (MWF) who \\npresents from ___ clinic with chest pain or shortness of \\nbreath, likely related demand ischemia in setting of dialysis.\\n\\nACUTE ISSUES\\n==============\\n#Chest Pain\\n#CAD - Patient initially presented with chest pain and shortness \\nof breath in the setting of dialysis, brought on after 3+ hours \\nof dialysis. Of note patient had recent hospitalization \\n(___) for similar complaints. EKG was similar to prior \\nwithout ischemic changes. Troponin peaked at 0.55, however \\nbaseline troponin is approximately 0.5, likely in the setting of \\nESRD and HFrEF. Shortness of breath and chest pain were likely \\nrelated to demand ischemia in the setting of dialysis. \\nAnti-anginals were up-titrated as part of medication \\noptimization. Home metoprolol XL was increased from 50 mg to 75 \\nmg BID. In addition home Imdur 120mg PO QD was initially reduced \\nand fractionated to Isordil 30 mg TID. She was continued on \\naspirin 81 mg PO QD, plavix 75 mg PO QD, atorvastatin 80 mg PO \\nQD, amlodipine 2.5 mg PO QHS, and SLNG PRN. Patient remained \\nchest pain-free without dyspnea during hemodialysis sessions \\nduring hospitalization. Per renal, will discharge patient on \\nincreased dose of home torsemide 40mg BID on non-dialysis days \\nand volume management per HD on HD days (MWF). DISCHARGE WEIGHT: \\n51.94 kg.\\n\\n#Anemia - Patient initially presented with hemoglobin of 6.8 \\nfrom a baseline of ___. She is status post 1 unit PRBC with \\npost-transfusion hemoglobin 9.5 after dialysis. There was no \\nobvious source of bleeding and she denied bloody stools or \\nhematuria. Stool guaiac was negative. Iron studies were \\nconsistent with anemia of chronic disease. There was no evidence \\nof hemolysis. Patient has a history of chronic anemia and was \\ncontinued on home ferrous sulfate 325 mg PO DAILY.\\n\\n#Tunneled Line Erythema - Patient was noted to have erythema and \\nmild tenderness to palpation around right chest tunneled line \\ninsertion site, with serous drainage. Swab cultures showed 1+ \\npolys with no growth to date. Blood cultures were also pending \\nat time of discharge. Patient remained afebrile without \\nleukocytosis.\\n\\n#HFrEF (EF 42%) - Per ED report, she appeared volume overloaded \\non arrival with BNP >33,000 chest x-ray showing new mild \\npulmonary edema. Patient is status post IV Lasix 120 mg in the \\nED with significant improvement on arrival to the floor. \\nTorsemide 20 mg PO BID was held on admission, with volume \\nmanagement per HD. She was discharged on torsemide 40BID on \\nnon-dialysis days.\\n\\nCHRONIC ISSUES\\n==============\\n#HTN - Continued on amlodipine 2.5 mg PO QHS and metoprolol 75 \\nXL BID per above\\n#S/P TAVR - Continued home Plavix 75 mg PO QD\\n#DM - On Lantus 5U QHS\\n#Hypothyroidism - Continued on home levothyroxine 50mcg ___ \\nand 100mcg ___\\n#COPD - Continued on mometasone-formoterol, montelukast, and \\nalbuterol inhalers\\n#GERD - Continued home Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H \\n#Galucoma - Continued home 0.005% Ophth. Soln. 1 DROP BOTH EYES \\nQHS \\n\\nTRANSITIONAL ISSUES\\n==================\\n[ ] NEW/CHANGED Medications\\n- Increased home torsemide to 40mg PO BID on non-dialysis days. \\nVolume management per HD on dialysis days\\n- Increased metoprolol succinate from 50 mg to 75 mg BID as part \\nof afterload reduction in order to help reduce demand ischemia\\n- Switched home Imdur 120mg PO QD to Isordil 90 mg QD given \\npatient likely had demand ischemia from dialysis and remained \\nchest pain free and normotensive on this dose during \\nhospitalization\\n\\n[ ] Follow-up pending blood cultures and dialysis catheter swab \\ncultures for possible line infection\\n\\nABNORMAL LABS ON DISCHARGE\\n- H/H 8.8/27.1, baseline Hb 8.0-9.0\\n- Creatinine 2.0\\n- LD 252\\n- Troponin 0.49, at baseline\\n- Phos 2.6\\n- Fe 36, ferritin 487, TIBC 176\\n\\nDISCHARGE WEIGHT: 51.94 kg\\n\\n# CONTACT: HCP: ___ (son) Phone: ___\\n# Code Status: Full Code\\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\nThe Preadmission Medication list is accurate and complete.\\n1. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY \\n2. Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM \\n3. Calcitriol 0.25 mcg PO M, WED, FRI \\n4. Clopidogrel 75 mg PO DAILY \\n5. Fluticasone Propionate 110mcg 2 PUFF IH DAILY \\n6. Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 2 SPRY NU DAILY \\n7. Levothyroxine Sodium 50 mcg PO ___ \\n8. Levothyroxine Sodium 100 mcg PO SUN \\n9. Metoprolol Succinate XL 50 mg PO BID \\n10. Montelukast 10 mg PO DAILY \\n11. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n12. Nitroglycerin SL 0.4 mg SL Q5MIN:PRN chest pain \\n13. Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H \\n14. albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/actuation inhalation Q6H:PRN \\n15. mometasone-formoterol 200-5 mcg/actuation inhalation BID \\n16. Ferrous Sulfate 325 mg PO DAILY \\n17. amLODIPine 2.5 mg PO QHS \\n18. Latanoprost 0.005% Ophth. Soln. 1 DROP BOTH EYES QHS \\n19. Isosorbide Mononitrate (Extended Release) 120 mg PO DAILY \\n20. Torsemide 20 mg PO BID \\n21. Potassium Chloride 30 mEq PO DAILY \\n22. Glargine 5 Units Bedtime\\n\\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1.  Isosorbide Mononitrate (Extended Release) 90 mg PO DAILY \\nRX *isosorbide mononitrate 30 mg 3 tablet(s) by mouth Daily Disp \\n#*30 Tablet Refills:*0 \\n2.  Torsemide 40 mg PO 4X/WEEK (___) \\nRX *torsemide [Demadex] 20 mg 2 tablet(s) by mouth Daily Disp \\n#*30 Tablet Refills:*0 \\n3.  Glargine 5 Units Bedtime \\n4.  Metoprolol Succinate XL 75 mg PO BID \\nRX *metoprolol succinate 50 mg 1.5 tablet(s) by mouth Daily Disp \\n#*30 Tablet Refills:*0 \\n5.  albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/actuation inhalation Q6H:PRN  \\n6.  amLODIPine 2.5 mg PO QHS  \\n7.  Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY  \\n8.  Atorvastatin 80 mg PO QPM  \\n9.  Calcitriol 0.25 mcg PO M, WED, FRI  \\n10.  Clopidogrel 75 mg PO DAILY  \\n11.  Ferrous Sulfate 325 mg PO DAILY  \\n12.  Fluticasone Propionate 110mcg 2 PUFF IH DAILY  \\n13.  Fluticasone Propionate NASAL 2 SPRY NU DAILY  \\n14.  Latanoprost 0.005% Ophth. Soln. 1 DROP BOTH EYES QHS  \\n15.  Levothyroxine Sodium 50 mcg PO ___  \\n16.  Levothyroxine Sodium 100 mcg PO SUN  \\n17.  mometasone-formoterol 200-5 mcg/actuation inhalation BID  \\n18.  Montelukast 10 mg PO DAILY  \\n19.  Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY  \\n20.  Nitroglycerin SL 0.4 mg SL Q5MIN:PRN chest pain  \\n21.  Pantoprazole 40 mg PO Q24H  \\n22.  Potassium Chloride 30 mEq PO DAILY  \\n\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nExtended Care\\n \\nFacility:\\n___\\n \\nDischarge Diagnosis:\\nPRIMARY DIAGNOSIS\\n- Chest Pain\\n- Anemia\\n\\nSECONDARY DIAGONSIS\\n- HFrEF\\n- HTN\\n- DM\\n- Hypothyroidism\\n- COPD\\n- GERD\\n- Chronic Anemia\\n- Glaucoma\\n\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nMental Status: Clear and coherent.\\nLevel of Consciousness: Alert and interactive.\\nActivity Status: Ambulatory - requires assistance or aid (walker \\nor cane).\\n\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nDear Ms. ___,\\n\\nIt was a pleasure taking care of you at ___ \\n___.\\n\\nYou initially came to the hospital because of worsening \\nshortness of breath and chest pain during dialysis.\\n\\nWhat happened during your hospitalization?\\n- You were anemic on admission, and received 1 unit of blood. \\nYour blood levels subsequently remained stable afterwards. There \\nwas no evidence of blood in your stool\\n- You continued to receive dialysis during your hospitalization\\n- Your metoprolol XL was increased to 75 mg twice daily and your \\ntorsemide was increased to 40mg twice daily. Please take your \\ntorsemide on non-dialysis days and you do not need to take the \\ntorsemide on the days you do have dialysis\\n\\nContinuing with the hospital?\\n- Continue to take all of your medications as prescribed\\n- Follow-up with your primary care physician ___ 1 week\\n- Follow-up with your primary cardiologist within 1 week\\n- Please keep all of your other scheduled appointments as listed \\nbelow\\n\\nIt was a pleasure taking care of you at ___ \\n___.\\n\\nSincerely,\\n\\nYour ___ Care Team\\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n',\n",
              " ' \\nName:  ___                Unit No:   ___\\n \\nAdmission Date:  ___              Discharge Date:   ___\\n \\nDate of Birth:  ___             Sex:   M\\n \\nService: MEDICINE\\n \\nAllergies: \\nFludara / Quinine / Rituxan / Gemzar\\n \\nAttending: ___.\\n \\nChief Complaint:\\nRLE Pain and weakness.\\n \\nMajor Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\\nNone.\\n \\nHistory of Present Illness:\\nTime of Initial Eval: ___ 23:30\\n\\nThe patient is a ___ y/o M with extensive oncologic history, \\nincluding B-cell lymphoma w/ spine mets and reported lesion on R \\ntibial plateau, as well a more recently-diagnosed esophageal \\nadenocarcinoma, who presents with worsening right knee pain and \\nright leg weakness, transferred to ___ for radiation oncology.\\n\\nPer report, pt has a known right tibial plateau lesion, \\npresumably related to involvement of his B-cell lymphoma, since \\n___. He had a fall in ___ and, since that time, has had \\nincreasing difficulty with ambulation. Over the day, he has \\nnoted increased worsening of his symptoms and contacted his \\noutpatient oncologist (Dr. ___ at ___. Because his \\nusual radiation oncologist a ___ is out of town, he was \\nreferred to the ___ ED for more urgent palliative radiation to \\nthe right knee. Of note, pt w/ recent d/c from ___ for \\ntransfusion, most recent chemo 3 weeks ago. \\n\\n___ w/ recurrent B-cell lymphoma w/ spine mets and reported \\nlesion on R tibial plateau, presents w/ worsening R knee pain \\nand weakness. Pt advised by his oncologist, Dr. ___, to \\ncome to ED for admission to irradiate knee palliatively. Pt w/ \\nrecent d/c from ___ for transfusion, most recent chemo 3 \\nweeks ago. Ambulating w/ walker since fall in ___, taking \\nPO dilaudid 6mg and tylenol at home. No incontinence. \\n\\nPE:\\nGen: Cachectic, A&O X3.\\nCard: RRR, no m/g/r\\nAbd: NT, ND\\nKnee: R knee w/ sig effusion, exquisite tenderness over medial \\ntibial head. \\n\\nDiagnosis: Knee pain likely ___ metastatic dz\\n\\nED Course (labs, imaging, interventions, consults): \\n- Initial Vitals/Trigger: 98 100 ___ 100% \\n\\nDisposition/Pending: \\n___ w/ multiple mets from recurrent B-cell lymphoma, presents w/ \\nknee pain, will be admitted for radiation tx. \\n-Chem 7, CBC w/ diff, pt not neutropenic\\n-Admit to ___ for radiation eval by Dr. ___, who is \\naware\\n-No acute cord compression per conversation w/ rads resident \\n___\\n\\nED Course:\\nInitial VS: ___ 100% \\nLabs significant for Na 132, WBC 3.0, Hct 28.9, LDH 405.\\nImaging: MRI C/T/L spine showing bony metastases throughout the \\nentire spine, no evidence of cord compression\\nMeds given: Tylenol, dilaudid\\nVS prior to transfer: 97.8 86 118/78 16 100% \\n \\nOn arrival to the floor, the patient reports continued right \\nknee pain, particularly on the medial aspect of the right tibial \\nplateau. He also endorses constipation and neuropathy in the \\ntoes, both of which are chronic for him. Has also reports some \\npressure sores on his back, which are uncomfotable for him. He \\nalso reports feeling that his left leg has been weaker recently.\\n \\nROS: As above. Denies headache, lightheadedness, dizziness, sore \\nthroat, sinus congestion, chest pain, heart palpitations, \\nshortness of breath, cough, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, \\nconstipation, urinary symptoms, skin rash. The remainder of the \\nROS was negative. \\n \\nPast Medical History:\\n- B-cell lymphoma, diagnosed in ___\\n- Malignant melanoma status post wide local excision and left \\naxillary lymph node dissection\\n- Type 2 diabetes\\n- Hypertension\\n- Esophageal adenocarcinoma, diagnosed in ___\\n- Hemolytic anemia\\n\\nMost recent chemo regimen was taxotere/cisplatin; however, pt \\nreports that his oncologist is planning on changing regimen to \\ncytoxan.\\n \\nSocial History:\\n___\\nFamily History:\\nGrandmother with colon CA. Uncle with esophageal CA. Another \\nuncle with multiple myeloma. Also reports a family history of \\nearly death from CAD.\\n \\nPhysical Exam:\\nADMISSION EXAM:\\nVS - 98.8 117/70 94 18 98%RA\\nGEN - Cachectic, NAD\\nHEENT - NC/AT, EOMI, OP clear\\nNECK - Supple, no cervical LAD\\nCV - RRR, no m/r/g\\nRESP - CTA B\\nABD - S/NT/ND, BS present\\nEXT - notable for swelling of the right knee, TTP over the \\nmedial aspect of the right tibial plateau\\nSKIN - no obvious skin rashes\\nNEURO - CN ___ grossly intact; diffuse muscular atrophy; ___ \\nstrength in the bilateral upper extremities; 4+/5 strength in \\nleft hip and knee flexion; ___ strength in left knee extension \\nand left ankle dorsiflexion; ___ strength in right lower \\nextremity; bilateral patellar reflexes intact\\nPSYCH - calm, appropriate\\n \\nPertinent Results:\\nADMISSION LABS:\\n___ 04:20PM BLOOD WBC-3.0* RBC-2.70* Hgb-8.9* Hct-28.9* \\nMCV-107* MCH-33.0* MCHC-30.8* RDW-22.1* Plt Ct-74*\\n___ 04:20PM BLOOD Neuts-83.9* Lymphs-9.3* Monos-6.2 Eos-0.6 \\nBaso-0\\n___ 04:20PM BLOOD Glucose-206* UreaN-16 Creat-0.5 Na-132* \\nK-4.5 Cl-97 HCO3-25 AnGap-15\\n___ 04:20PM BLOOD LD(LDH)-405*\\n___ 04:20PM BLOOD Calcium-8.8 Phos-3.3 Mg-1.8 UricAcd-4.3\\n.\\n___ MRI C/T/L-SPINE: IMPRESSION:\\n1. Degenerative findings as above, with no evidence of severe \\nspinal canal narrowing.\\n2.  Innumerable foci of abnormal signal throughout the bone \\nmarrow of the cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine, concerning \\nfor widespread neoplastic disease.\\n3. Large right and small left pleural effusions.\\n.\\n___ RIGHT TIB/FIB X-RAY: IMPRESSION: Atherosclerotic \\nvascular calcifications within the lower leg.  Moderate-sized \\nknee effusion.  No acute fractures or dislocations. Tiny \\npatellar osteophytes.  No definite lesion identified.  There is \\nsubtle mottled lesion within the medial tibial plateau.  \\nCorrelate with outside hospital MRI.  No cortical thinning or \\ncortical destruction or tibial periostitis.\\n.\\n___ MRI PELVIS: PRELIM!!: Non-diagnostic.\\n.\\nDISCHARGE LABS:\\n___ 07:00AM BLOOD WBC-2.0* RBC-2.20* Hgb-7.2* Hct-22.9* \\nMCV-104* MCH-32.6* MCHC-31.4 RDW-20.1* Plt Ct-62*\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD Neuts-71.5* ___ Monos-8.6 Eos-0.7 \\nBaso-0.2\\n___ 09:00AM BLOOD Hypochr-3+ Anisocy-2+ Poiklo-2+ \\nMacrocy-3+ Microcy-OCCASIONAL Polychr-1+ Ovalocy-1+ Tear Dr-1+\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD Hypochr-NORMAL Anisocy-1+ Poiklo-NORMAL \\nMacrocy-2+ Microcy-NORMAL Polychr-1+\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD ___ PTT-33.0 ___\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD Ret Aut-7.5*\\n___ 07:00AM BLOOD Glucose-110* UreaN-14 Creat-0.3* Na-131* \\nK-4.0 Cl-99 HCO3-25 AnGap-11\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD ALT-11 AST-19 AlkPhos-100 TotBili-2.1*# \\nDirBili-0.6* IndBili-1.5\\n___ 07:00AM BLOOD ALT-14 AST-19 LD(LDH)-435* AlkPhos-105 \\nTotBili-1.8*\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD Albumin-3.0* Calcium-8.3* Phos-3.1 Mg-1.7\\n___ 06:45AM BLOOD VitB12-568 Folate-13.9 Hapto-<5*\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\n___ man with extensive oncologic history, including B-cell \\nlymphoma w/ spine mets and reported lesion on right tibial \\nplateau, as well a more recently-diagnosed esophageal \\nadenocarcinoma, who presents with worsening right knee pain and \\nbilateral leg weakness, transferred to ___ for radiation \\noncology.\\n\\n# Right leg pain: MRI from ___ showed a \\nmetastatic deposit.  Radiation Oncology consulted.  Plan for \\noutpatient XRT.  Increased hydromorphone PRN and standing \\nacetaminophen.  Recommended long-acting narcotic, but Mr. \\n___ refused.\\n.\\n# New bilateral lower extremity weakness: Weakness started last \\n___.  Unable to walk.  No change in bowel or bladder \\nfunction.  MRI spine negative for cord compression.  Weakness \\nstarted last ___.  Outpatient oncologist Dr. ___ \\n___ that this is the ___ time Mr. ___ has had this \\nhappen and he has already had XRT and cyberknife.  Neurosurgery \\nand Neuro-onc consulted.  No role for surgery.  LP recommended \\nto rule out CNS involvement, but Mr. ___ refused.  MRI of \\nlumbosacral plexus with STIR and gadolinium to rule out cauda \\nequina syndrome or lumbosacral plexus involvement: PRELIM report \\nnon-diagnostic.  Continued low-dose dexamethasone.  Dr. \\n___ wanted to avoid any dose escalation due to Mr. \\n___ chronic cachexia and diabetes, which he believes are \\nrelated; so the steroid dose was not changed.  Physical therapy. \\n Mr. ___ threatened to leave AMA, but waited until he had \\nthe MRIs done.\\n.\\n# B-cell lymphoma / esophageal adenocarcinoma: Followed by Dr. \\n___ at ___.  Most recently, he was on \\ndocetaxel/cisplatin, soon to be transitioned to \\ncyclophosphamide.  Continued home dexamethasone.  Continued home \\nTMP-SMX PPx.  Continued outpatient standing ondansetron.\\n.\\n# Pancytopenia: Macrocytic.  Possibly due marrow involvement \\nand/or chemo-induced myelosuppression (3 weeks ago).  Normal B12 \\nand folate levels.  High retic count, indirect \\nhyperbilirubinemia, and undetectable haptoglobin favor hemolytic \\nanemia.  Direct Coombs negative.  Mr. ___ declined RBC \\ntransfusion stating he only gets transfused for HCT <20; his HCT \\nwas 22.9.\\n.\\n# Abnormal LFTs: Indirect hyperbilirubinemia consistent with \\nhemolysis.\\n.\\n# Hyponatremia: Hypovolemic by history.  Mild.  Given IV fluids, \\nbut no improvement.  Urine Na 114 consistent with SIADH.\\n.\\n# T2DM: Continued outpatient glipizide.  Refused diabetic diet.\\n.\\n# HTN: Continued outpatient propranolol and low-dose aspirin.\\n.\\n# Constipation: Continued docusate, senna, polyethylene glycol.  \\nFleets enema PRN.\\n.\\n# Anxiety: Lorazepam PRN.\\n.\\n# FEN: Regular diet (refused diabetic low-sodium diet).\\n.\\n# GI PPx: PPI with steroids.  Bowel regimen as above.\\n.\\n# DVT PPx: Heparin SC.\\n.\\n# Precautions: Fall.\\n.\\n# CODE: FULL (confirmed).\\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\nThe Preadmission Medication list may be inaccurate and requires \\nfuther investigation.\\n1. Ondansetron 8 mg PO Q 8H nausea \\n2. HYDROmorphone (Dilaudid) 6 mg PO Q3H pain \\n3. Sulfameth/Trimethoprim DS 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n4. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY \\n5. Omeprazole 20 mg PO BID \\n6. FoLIC Acid 1 mg PO DAILY \\n7. GlipiZIDE XL 10 mg PO BID \\n8. Propranolol 10 mg PO DAILY \\nhold for HR<60 \\n9. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO Q6H pain \\n10. Diazepam 5 mg PO PRN anxiety \\npt reports that he rarely takes this \\n11. Dexamethasone 1 mg PO DAILY \\n12. Lorazepam 0.5 mg PO PRN anxiety \\nrarely take this \\n13. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n14. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO DAILY \\n15. Senna 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n16. Epoetin Alfa 0 unites SC WEEKLY Start: HS \\n17. Filgrastim Dose is Unknown  SC Q24H \\nafter chemo \\n18. Megestrol Acetate Dose is Unknown  PO DAILY \\n19. Polyethylene Glycol 17 g PO DAILY \\n20. Fleet Enema ___AILY:PRN constipation\\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1. Acetaminophen 1000 mg PO Q6H pain \\n2. Aspirin 81 mg PO DAILY \\n3. Dexamethasone 1 mg PO DAILY \\n4. Fleet Enema ___AILY:PRN constipation \\n5. FoLIC Acid 1 mg PO DAILY \\n6. GlipiZIDE XL 10 mg PO BID \\n7. Lorazepam 0.5 mg PO Q4H:PRN Nausea, anxiety, insomnia \\n8. Multivitamins 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n9. Omeprazole 20 mg PO BID \\n10. Ondansetron 8 mg PO Q 8H nausea \\n11. Polyethylene Glycol 17 g PO DAILY \\n12. Propranolol 10 mg PO DAILY \\nhold for HR<60 \\n13. Senna 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n14. Sulfameth/Trimethoprim DS 1 TAB PO DAILY \\n15. Vitamin D ___ UNIT PO DAILY \\n16. Diazepam 5 mg PO PRN anxiety \\npt reports that he rarely takes this \\n17. HYDROmorphone (Dilaudid) ___ mg PO Q2H:PRN pain \\n18. Megestrol Acetate 400 mg PO DAILY \\n19. testosterone *NF* 1 patch TRANSDERMAL DAILY\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nHome With Service\\n \\nFacility:\\n___\\n \\n___:\\nMetastatic lymphoma.\\nMetastatic esophageal cancer.\\nNew leg weakness (right worse than left).\\nPain due to cancer metastasis.\\nPancytopenia (low blood counts).\\nHemolytic anemia (low red blood cell count due to destruction of \\nred blood cells).\\nDiabetes.\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nMental Status: Clear and coherent.\\nLevel of Consciousness: Alert and interactive.\\nActivity Status: Bedbound.\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nYou were admitted to the hospital for worsening leg weakness and \\nright knee pain.  An MRI of your spine did not show spinal cord \\ncompression.  You were seen by the spine surgeons, \\nneuro-oncology, and radiation oncology.  Radition to the right \\nknee can be done as an outpatient closer to home.  An MRI of the \\npelvis did not show a cause of your leg weakness, but this is \\nonly a PRELIMINARY report and your doctor ___ need to ___ \\nthe final report.  You refused a lumbar puncture (spinal tap), \\nso the cause of your leg weakness could not be determined.  You \\nwill need a blood transfusion soon, but did not want one while \\nyou were in the hospital.\\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n']"
