              "summary": "{\n  \"name\": \"notes_df\",\n  \"rows\": 2000,\n  \"fields\": [\n    {\n      \"column\": \"subject_id\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 2842814,\n        \"min\": 10014354,\n        \"max\": 19998330,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 1869,\n        \"samples\": [\n          11178953,\n          13793264,\n          17686891\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"hadm_id\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 2892811,\n        \"min\": 20001504,\n        \"max\": 29996653,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 2000,\n        \"samples\": [\n          23508217,\n          29146367,\n          21534019\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"charttime\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"object\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 1948,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"2171-03-25 00:00:00\",\n          \"2115-10-03 00:00:00\",\n          \"2137-06-23 00:00:00\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"text\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 2000,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \" \\nName:  ___                  Unit No:   ___\\n \\nAdmission Date:  ___              Discharge Date:   ___\\n \\nDate of Birth:  ___             Sex:   F\\n \\nService: MEDICINE\\n \\nAllergies: \\nNo Known Allergies / Adverse Drug Reactions\\n \\nAttending: ___.\\n \\nChief Complaint:\\n\\\"I don't want to take my lantus\\\" leading to hyperglycemia \\n \\nMajor Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\\nnone\\n\\n \\nHistory of Present Illness:\\n___ with PMH of HTN, T2DM p/w elevated blood glucose 300s and \\ngeneral feeling of tiredness, sleepy, HA for the past few days. \\nPt had dx of possible PNA vs inflammation in lungs on ___ and \\nwas given dose of levaquin + 5days azithro. Pt says she still \\nfeels congested with cough and clear sputum. ___ 274 in ED. Given \\nIVF in ED and admitted to medicine for ___ and dehydration as \\nwell as hyperglycemia.  \\n.  \\nVital signs prior to transfer were 98.1po 93 118/74 18 100%ra  \\n.  \\n.  \\nROS: Endorses chronic low back pain.  \\n \\nPast Medical History:\\nPSYCHIATRIC HISTORY:\\n* Psychiatrist Dr. ___ @ ___ Therapist ___ @ ___ has not seen for 2 months\\n* Diagnosed as paranoid schizophrenia vs bipolar do, opiate and\\nbenzo depend. with cluster B traits\\n* hx of multiple psychiatric admissions, @ ___ ___, also @\\n___ and ___ in the past, last ___ discharge ___ the patient almost cut off her hand in the context of\\ndistress at her former husband's abuse\\n* Habit management ___ clinic ___\\n* Bridge to Recovery ___ on ___\\n\\nPAST MEDICAL HISTORY:\\n* PCP ___ @ ___\\n* Diabetes\\n* HTN\\n* Arthritis\\n* chronic low back and leg pain\\n* MVA ___ then in a whole body cast\\n* hx of injured shoulder\\n \\nSocial History:\\n___\\nFamily History:\\nShe grew up in foster care so she does not know what diseases \\nher family has. \\n \\nPhysical Exam:\\nADMISSION PHYSICAL EXAM:  \\nVS:96.3 120/85 84 16 98RA  \\nGENERAL: Middle aged woman sleeping comfortably in NAD  \\nHEENT: NC/AT  \\nNECK: Supple  \\nHEART: RRR, no MRG, nl S1-S2.  \\nLUNGS: CTA bilat  \\nABDOMEN: Soft/NT/ND, no masses or HSM, no rebound/guarding.  \\nEXTREMITIES: WWP, no c/c/e, 2+ peripheral pulses.  \\nSKIN: No rashes or lesions.  \\n  \\nDISCHARGE PHYSICAL EXAM:  \\nunchanged\\n\\n \\nPertinent Results:\\nLABS:\\n  \\nAdmission:\\n___ 09:44PM BLOOD WBC-7.0# RBC-4.36 Hgb-12.7 Hct-37.0 \\nMCV-85 MCH-29.1 MCHC-34.4 RDW-14.4 Plt ___\\n___ 09:44PM BLOOD Neuts-44.0* Lymphs-47.6* Monos-5.1 \\nEos-2.6 Baso-0.8\\n___ 09:44PM BLOOD Glucose-241* UreaN-27* Creat-1.9* Na-139 \\nK-3.9 Cl-101 HCO3-28 AnGap-14\\n___ 09:48PM BLOOD Glucose-223* K-4.0\\n  \\nDischarge:\\n___ 06:25AM BLOOD WBC-5.9 RBC-4.16* Hgb-12.4 Hct-35.6* \\nMCV-86 MCH-29.8 MCHC-34.8 RDW-14.2 Plt ___\\n___ 06:25AM BLOOD Glucose-183* UreaN-27* Creat-1.3* Na-138 \\nK-4.0 Cl-100 HCO3-29 AnGap-13\\n___ 06:25AM BLOOD Calcium-9.8 Phos-4.1 Mg-1.9\\n  \\nIMAGING:\\nThe linear opacities noted at the right lung base have improved \\nto near resolution on the current study. No definite focal \\nconsolidation or superimposed edema noted.\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\n___ ___ with history of Bipolar vs Schizophrenia, DM, recent PNA \\npresenting with chief complaint of not wanting to take her \\nlantus, found to be hyperglycemic with acute kidney injury.\\n \\nACTIVE ISSUES:  \\n   \\nHyperglycemia: Secondary to self-reported insulin \\nnon-compliance.  She says that her PCP recently switched her to \\nlong acting lantus rather than the humulin 70/30 that she had \\nused previously, and she did not feel that the lantus worked as \\nwell, so she stopped using it.  Fingerstick was 274 in the ED, \\nreached the 300s shortly after admission.  No evidence of DKA or \\nhyperosmolar state. She was treated with lantus + sliding scale \\ninsulin, however her sugars remained in the 200s due to diet \\nnon-compliance, i.e. she ate multiple ice creams, ___ ices, \\nand ___ crackers in the kitchen in addition to full meals.  \\nBecause of her reluctance to take lantus, she was discharged on \\nher previous dose of 70/30 so that she would be getting some \\nform of insulin. Her metformin had been held on admission, but \\nshe was advised to continue this at home.  She was also given \\nnumerous materials on the components of a proper diabetic diet \\nand instructed to follow up with her PCP.  \\n   \\n___: Cr to 1.9 on admission from baseline of 1.0, likely \\npre-renal in setting of dehydration ___ hyperglycemia.  \\nLisinopril was held on admission.  She was given fluids \\novernight and PO hydration, and her Cr improved to 1.3 the \\nfollowing morning.  She was instructed to restart her lisinopril \\non discharge, however she was counseled on the importance of \\nkeeping her sugars better controlled and staying well-hydrated \\nto prevent acute renal injury again.\\n  \\nChronic back pain:   She was not given oxycodone during the \\nadmission, as her PCP has refused to prescribe this in the past. \\n He referred her to pain clinic, which she DNK'd. She threatened \\nto leave AMA after we refused to give her oxycodone   We had a \\nfrank conversation with her about this, and discussed that \\nnarcotic pain medications are often not the best treatment for \\nchronic pain.  We again stressed the importance of being seen in \\nthe pain clinic.  She was discharged with non-narcotic pain meds \\nfor her chronic back pain.\\n \\nAbnormal U/A: Initial UA suggested of possible infection, \\nhowever likely contaminated urine given epithelial cells.  Given \\n1 dose cipro overnight, UA was repeated and showed no sign of \\ninfection.  Not discharged with any antibiotics.\\n  \\nCHRONIC ISSUES:\\n   \\nSchizophrenia/Bipolar: Stable, continued on her outpatient psych \\nmedications.\\n  \\nTRANSFER OF CARE ISSUES:\\n1. DM: assess glycemic control on 70/30 regimen   \\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\n1. aripiprazole 15 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY  \\n(Daily).  \\n2. clonazepam 1 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO QHS (once a day \\n \\n(at bedtime)).  \\n3. clonidine 0.1 mg Tablet Sig: Three (3) Tablet PO DAILY  \\n(Daily).  \\n4. gabapentin 400 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO TID (3  \\ntimes a day).  \\n5. aspirin 81 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable  \\n\\nPO DAILY (Daily).  \\n6. hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO  \\nDAILY (Daily).  \\n7. lisinopril 20 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY (Daily). \\n \\n \\n8. mirtazapine 15 mg Tablet Sig: Three (3) Tablet PO HS (at  \\nbedtime).  \\n9. promethazine 25 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO DAILY  \\n(Daily).  \\n10. oxycodone 5 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO q6-8hrs:PRN  \\npain as needed for pain for 5 days.  \\nDisp:*10 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n11. guaifenesin 100 mg/5 mL Syrup Sig: ___ MLs PO Q6H (every 6  \\n\\nhours) as needed for cough for 7 days.  \\nDisp:*250 ML(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n12. nicotine 14 mg/24 hr Patch 24 hr Sig: One (1) Patch 24 hr  \\nTransdermal DAILY (Daily) for 5 days.  \\nDisp:*5 Patch 24 hr(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n13. acetaminophen 500 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO TID (3  \\ntimes a day).  \\n14. ranitidine HCl 150 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2  \\ntimes a day).  \\nDisp:*20 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n15. ibuprofen 400 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO Q6H (every 6  \\n\\nhours) for 7 days.  \\nDisp:*28 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n16. azithromycin 250 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY  \\n(Daily) for 3 days.  \\nDisp:*3 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n17. insulin lispro protam & lispro 100 unit/mL (75-25)  \\nSuspension Sig: ___ (25) units Subcutaneous with  \\nbreakfast.  \\n18. insulin lispro protam & lispro 100 unit/mL (75-25)  \\nSuspension Sig: Ten (10) units Subcutaneous with dinner.  \\n19. metformin 1,000 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO twice a day \\n \\nas needed for pain.  \\n20. benzonatate 100 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO BID:PRN  \\nas needed for cough for 7 days.  \\nDisp:*14 Capsule(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n21. Tylenol ___ mg Tablet Sig: Three (3) Tablet PO TID:PRN as  \\nneeded for pain for 5 days.  \\nDisp:*45 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*  \\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1. aripiprazole 15 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO DAILY \\n(Daily).  \\n2. clonidine 0.1 mg Tablet Sig: Three (3) Tablet PO DAILY \\n(Daily).  \\n3. gabapentin 400 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO TID (3 \\ntimes a day).  \\n4. aspirin 81 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable \\nPO DAILY (Daily).  \\n5. hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg Capsule Sig: One (1) Capsule PO \\nDAILY (Daily).  \\n6. lisinopril 20 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO once a day.  \\n7. mirtazapine 15 mg Tablet Sig: Three (3) Tablet PO at bedtime. \\n \\n8. promethazine 25 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO DAILY \\n(Daily).  \\n9. nicotine 14 mg/24 hr Patch 24 hr Sig: One (1) Patch 24 hr \\nTransdermal DAILY (Daily).  \\n10. Acetaminophen Extra Strength 500 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) \\nTablet PO TID (3 times a day).  \\n11. ranitidine HCl 150 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2 \\ntimes a day).  \\n12. metformin 1,000 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO twice a \\nday.  \\n13. acetaminophen 325 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO three \\ntimes a day as needed for pain.  \\n14. insulin NPH & regular human 100 unit/mL (70-30) Suspension \\nSig: ___ (45) units Subcutaneous once a day: with \\nbreakfast.  \\n15. Humulin 70/30 100 unit/mL (70-30) Suspension Sig: Ten (10) \\nunits Subcutaneous with dinner.  \\n16. albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/Actuation HFA Aerosol Inhaler Sig: \\nOne (1) puff Inhalation every four (4) hours as needed for \\nshortness of breath or wheezing.  \\n\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nHome\\n \\nDischarge Diagnosis:\\nPrimary diagnosis:\\nDiabetes mellitus type II\\nAcute kidney injury\\n\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nMental Status: Clear and coherent.\\nLevel of Consciousness: Alert and interactive.\\nActivity Status: Ambulatory - Independent.\\n\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nDear Ms. ___,\\n \\nIt was a pleasure to take care of you during your hospital stay \\nat ___.  You were admitted to the hospital because of high \\nsugars from not taking your insulin and mild kidney damage from \\ndehydration.  You improved after getting insulin and fluids.\\n \\nIt is very, very important that you always remember to take your \\ninsulin as directed and try to stay away from sugary, fatty \\nfoods in order to keep your blood sugars down.\\n  \\nChanges to your medications:\\nSTART insulin 70/30 45 units in AM, 10 units with dinner. You \\nwill need to check your fingersticks frequently to see if your \\nsugar drops.\\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n\",\n          \" \\nName:  ___                   Unit No:   ___\\n \\nAdmission Date:  ___              Discharge Date:   ___\\n \\nDate of Birth:  ___             Sex:   F\\n \\nService: SURGERY\\n \\nAllergies: \\nCodeine / Oxycodone / ciprofloxacin\\n \\nAttending: ___.\\n \\nChief Complaint:\\nfever, chills\\n \\nMajor Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\\nBedside incision and drainage ___\\n \\nHistory of Present Illness:\\nMs. ___ is a ___ year old Female s/p ventral hernia repair \\nwith mesh on ___. She tolerated this well but her postop \\ncourse was complicated by hospital acquired pneumonia for which \\nshe was given a 7 day course of levofloxacin to complete at \\nhome. She states that the levofloxacin caused nausea and \\nvomiting, so she only completed a 5 day course. Her nausea \\nresolved after stopping her antibiotic. She was then in her \\nusual state until 4 days ago\\nwhen she started having subjective fevers, chills, fatigue, and \\nlack of appetite. She states her temperature during these days \\nwas primarily 100.4 which she medicated with tylenol. She comes \\nto the ER today because of her persistent symptoms. She \\notherwise denies nausea, vomiting, following the period after \\nstopping her antibiotics. She has been passing flatus and having \\nnormal bowel movements. She has some abdominal pain which she \\nattributes to her incisions. She denies, weight loss, dizziness, \\nchest pain, shortness of breath, cough, dysuria, hematuria, \\nlower extremity edema, and new rashes.\\n \\nPast Medical History:\\nHTN\\nDM-2\\ndiverticulitis\\nhyperlipidemia\\nthalassemia trait\\npneumonia\\nurinary incontinence\\nostopenia\\nfatty liver\\ncolonic adenoma\\nlichen planus\\nobesity\\nhordeolum\\n\\n \\nSocial History:\\n___\\nFamily History:\\nnon-contributory\\n\\n \\nPhysical Exam:\\nVitals: Temp 99.2, HR 69, BP 121/60, RR 18\\nGen:NAD, AOX3\\nCV:RRR\\nResp:CTAB\\nAbd: wound vac in place, suctioning well.  When removed \\nappximately 3 inch length x1.5 inch width, 3inch depth well \\ngranulating wound on lower aspect of midline wound. Staples \\nremoved. Previous JP drain sites healing well without erythema\\nExt:No ___ edema\\n \\nPertinent Results:\\nCT ABD/PELVIS  w/ contrast ___: \\n1.  Large rim-enhancing fluid collection with tiny locules of \\nair and adjacent stranding in the anterior subcutaneous tissues \\nis concerning for abscess. \\n2.  Smaller intra-abdominal rim-enhancing loculated fluid \\ncollection may \\nrepresent intra-abdominal abscess.  \\n3.  Tiny focus of air in the bladder. Correlate with history of \\ninstrumentation.  \\n\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\nMs. ___ was admitted to the ___ surgery floor on ___. \\nShe was started on IV antibiotics for her wound infection. A CT \\nabd/pel showed a subcutaneous abscess.  On ___, she \\nunderwent bedside  incision and drainage of her abscess. \\nPurulent output was noted at this time. This was sent for \\nculture. Her wound was packed with dressing. She underwent wound \\nvac placement on ___.  Her wound cultures retuned with MRSA \\nsensitive to bactrim. She was discharged home with a 2 week \\ncourse of bactrim.  She was tolerating regular diet and her pain \\nwas controlled with tylenol at time of her discharge. She had no \\ncomplications or consultations during her hospital stay.\\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\nNiacin 1000 mg q day, Atenolol 12.5 mg BID, Metformin 1000mg \\nBID, Aspirin 81 mg q day\\n\\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1. atenolol 25 mg Tablet Sig: 0.5 Tablet PO BID (2 times a day). \\n \\n2. acetaminophen 325 mg Tablet Sig: Two (2) Tablet PO Q6H (every \\n6 hours) as needed for fever and pain.  \\n3. aspirin 81 mg Tablet, Chewable Sig: One (1) Tablet, Chewable \\nPO DAILY (Daily).  \\n4. metformin 500 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) Tablet PO BID (2 times a \\nday).  \\n5. ferrous sulfate 300  mg (60 mg iron) Tablet Sig: One (1) \\nTablet PO DAILY (Daily).\\nDisp:*30 Tablet(s)* Refills:*2*\\n6. sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim 800-160 mg Tablet Sig: One (1) \\nTablet PO BID (2 times a day).\\nDisp:*28 Tablet(s)* Refills:*0*\\n\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nHome With Service\\n \\nFacility:\\n___\\n \\nDischarge Diagnosis:\\nWound infection, wound abscess\\n\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nMental Status: Clear and coherent.\\nLevel of Consciousness: Alert and interactive.\\nActivity Status: Ambulatory - Independent.\\n\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nDear Ms. ___, \\nyou were admitted for management of a wound infection and \\nabscess collection along your recent hernia incision.  Your \\nincision was opened and wound cultures were sent to microbiology \\nwhich grew out a bacteria known as MRSA (Methicillin resistant \\nstaph aureus).  This organism can be treated with bactrim, which \\nyou currently are on. You will need to continue taking this \\nmedication for a short time after discharge. \\nYou had a wound vac placed on your abdomen which will need to be \\nin place for several weeks. A wound care nurse ___ come to your \\nhouse to change put one on today.  It will be changed again on \\n___, and then again on ___ at your appointment with \\n___.  ***Please make sure to bring your wound vac supplies \\n(sponge and tape in packaging to your appointment with Dr. ___ \\non ___\\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n\",\n          \" \\nName:  ___                    Unit No:   ___\\n \\nAdmission Date:  ___              Discharge Date:   ___\\n \\nDate of Birth:  ___             Sex:   F\\n \\nService: NEUROSURGERY\\n \\nAllergies: \\nSulfa (Sulfonamide Antibiotics) / morphine\\n \\nAttending: ___.\\n \\nChief Complaint:\\nAneurysm coiling\\n \\nMajor Surgical or Invasive Procedure:\\n___- Coiling of the ACOM aneurysm.\\n\\n \\nHistory of Present Illness:\\n___ y/o female with incidental finding of a Acom aneurysm who \\npresented today for elective coiling. The risks and benefits of \\nsurgical intervention were discussed and the patient consented \\nto the procedure. \\n \\nPast Medical History:\\nMS\\n___\\n \\nSocial History:\\n___\\nFamily History:\\nNC\\n \\nPhysical Exam:\\nPHYSICAL EXAMINATION ON DISCHARGE:\\nAlert and oriented x3. Speech fluent and clear. Comprehension \\nintact.\\nCN II-XII grossly intact.\\nMotor strength examination ___ in the upper and lower \\nextremities bilaterally.\\nGroin site: + ecchymosis; soft and non-tender.\\n \\nBrief Hospital Course:\\nMs. ___ presented to the hospital to undergo an elective \\naneurysm coiling under general anesthesia. Procedure was \\nuncomplicated, patient was extubated in the angio suite and \\nbrought to the ICU for monitoing. \\n\\nOn ___, the patient remained stable and was transferred to the \\nfloor for one additional day of observation. Overnight, she \\nexperienced one episode of hypotension with a SBP in the ___ and \\na fall without head strike. Her BP returned to normal limits \\nwithout intervention.\\n\\nOn ___, the patient remained neurologically stable. She was \\nambulating independently, tolerating a diet and voiding without \\ndifficulty. It was determined she would be discharged to home \\ntoday. \\n\\n \\nMedications on Admission:\\nThe Preadmission Medication list may be inaccurate and requires \\nfuther investigation.\\n1. Naproxen 500 mg PO Q12H:PRN pain. \\n\\n \\nDischarge Medications:\\n1. Amlodipine 5 mg PO DAILY \\n2. BuPROPion (Sustained Release) 100 mg PO BID \\n3. Citalopram 20 mg PO DAILY \\n4. Lisinopril 20 mg PO BID \\n5. Betaseron (interferon beta-1b) 0.3 mg subcutaneous Every \\nother day \\n6. Acetaminophen 325-650 mg PO Q6H:PRN fever or pain \\nDo not exceed greater than 4g Acetaminophen in a 24-hour period. \\n \\n7. OxycoDONE (Immediate Release)  ___ mg PO Q4H:PRN pain \\nHold for sedation, drowsiness, or RR <12. Do not drive while \\ntaking this medication.  \\nRX *oxycodone 5 mg ___ tablet(s) by mouth every 4 hours Disp \\n#*30 Tablet Refills:*0\\n8. Aspirin 325 mg PO DAILY \\nRX *aspirin 325 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth DAILY Disp #*30 Tablet \\nRefills:*3\\n9. Docusate Sodium 100 mg PO BID \\nRX *docusate sodium 100 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day Disp \\n#*40 Tablet Refills:*0\\n10. Hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg PO DAILY \\n11. MetFORMIN (Glucophage) 500 mg PO BID \\n\\n \\nDischarge Disposition:\\nHome\\n \\nDischarge Diagnosis:\\nACOM Aneurysm\\n\\n \\nDischarge Condition:\\nMental Status: Clear and coherent.\\nLevel of Consciousness: Alert and interactive.\\nActivity Status: Ambulatory - Independent.\\n\\n \\nDischarge Instructions:\\nDr. ___ \\n___ \\n\\nActivity\\n\\u0095You may gradually return to your normal activities, but we \\nrecommend you take it easy for the next ___ hours to avoid \\nbleeding from your groin.\\n\\u0095Heavy lifting, running, climbing, or other strenuous exercise \\nshould be avoided for ten (10) days. This is to prevent bleeding \\nfrom your groin.\\n\\u0095You make take leisurely walks and slowly increase your \\nactivity at your own pace. ___ try to do too much all at once.\\n\\u0095Do not go swimming or submerge yourself in water for five (5) \\ndays after your procedure.\\n\\u0095You make take a shower.\\n\\nMedications\\n\\u0095Resume your normal medications and begin new medications as \\ndirected.\\n\\u0095You may be instructed by your doctor to take one ___ a day \\nand/or Plavix. If so, do not take any other products that have \\naspirin in them. If you are unsure of what products contain \\nAspirin, as your pharmacist or call our office.\\n\\u0095You may use Acetaminophen (Tylenol) for minor discomfort if \\nyou are not otherwise restricted from taking this medication.\\n\\u0095If you take Metformin (Glucophage) you may start it again \\nthree (3) days after your procedure.\\n\\nCare of the Puncture Site\\n\\u0095You will have a small bandage over the site.\\n\\u0095Remove the bandage in 24 hours by soaking it with water and \\ngently peeling it off.\\n\\u0095Keep the site clean with soap and water and dry it carefully.\\n\\u0095You may use a band-aid if you wish.\\n\\nWhat You ___ Experience:\\n\\u0095Mild tenderness and bruising at the puncture site (groin).\\n\\u0095Soreness in your arms from the intravenous lines.\\n\\u0095Mild to moderate headaches that last several days to a few \\nweeks.\\n\\u0095Fatigue is very normal.\\n\\u0095Constipation is common. Be sure to drink plenty of fluids and \\neat a high-fiber diet. If you are taking narcotics (prescription \\npain medications), try an over-the-counter stool softener.\\n\\nWhen to Call Your Doctor at ___ for:\\n\\u0095Severe pain, swelling, redness or drainage from the puncture \\nsite. \\n\\u0095Fever greater than 101.5 degrees Fahrenheit\\n\\u0095Constipation\\n\\u0095Blood in your stool or urine\\n\\u0095Nausea and/or vomiting\\n\\u0095Extreme sleepiness and not being able to stay awake\\n\\u0095Severe headaches not relieved by pain relievers\\n\\u0095Seizures\\n\\u0095Any new problems with your vision or ability to speak\\n\\u0095Weakness or changes in sensation in your face, arms, or leg\\n\\n \\nFollowup Instructions:\\n___\\n\"\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"clean_text\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 2000,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"name: unit no:\\n\\nadmission date: discharge date:\\n\\ndate of birth: sex: f\\n\\nservice: medicine\\n\\nallergies:\\nno known allergies / adverse drug reactions\\n\\nattending: .\\n\\nchief complaint:\\n\\\"i don't want to take my lantus\\\" leading to hyperglycemia\\n\\nmajor surgical or invasive procedure:\\nnone\\n\\nhistory of present illness:\\n with pmh of htn, type 2 diabetes p/w elevated blood glucose 300s and\\ngeneral feeling of tiredness, sleepy, ha for the past few days.\\npt had dx of possible pna vs inflammation in lungs on and\\nwas given dose of levaquin + 5days azithro. pt says she still\\nfeels congested with cough and clear sputum. 274 in ed. given\\nivf in ed and admitted to medicine for and dehydration as\\nwell as hyperglycemia.\\n.\\nvital signs prior to transfer were 98.1po 93 118/74 18 100%ra\\n.\\n.\\nros: endorses chronic low back pain.\\n\\npast medical history:\\npsychiatric history:\\n* psychiatrist dr. @ therapist @ has not seen for 2 months\\n* diagnosed as paranoid schizophrenia vs bipolar do, opiate and\\nbenzo depend. with cluster b traits\\n* hx of multiple psychiatric admissions, @ , also @\\n and in the past, last discharge the patient almost cut off her hand in the context of\\ndistress at her former husband's abuse\\n* habit management clinic\\n* bridge to recovery on\\n\\npast medical history:\\n* pcp @\\n* diabetes mellitus\\n* htn\\n* arthritis\\n* chronic low back and leg pain\\n* mva then in a whole body cast\\n* hx of injured shoulder\\n\\nsocial history:\\n\\nfamily history:\\nshe grew up in foster care so she does not know what diseases\\nher family has.\\n\\nphysical exam:\\nadmission physical exam:\\nvs:96.3 120/85 84 16 98ra\\ngeneral: middle aged woman sleeping comfortably in nad\\nheent: nc/at\\nneck: supple\\nheart: rrr, no mrg, nl s1-s2.\\nlungs: cta bilat\\nabdomen: soft/nt/nd, no masses or hsm, no rebound/guarding.\\nextremities: wwp, no c/c/e, 2+ peripheral pulses.\\nskin: no rashes or lesions.\\n\\ndischarge physical exam:\\nunchanged\\n\\npertinent results:\\nlabs:\\n\\nadmission:\\n 09:44pm blood wbc-7.0# rbc-4.36 hgb-12.7 hct-37.0\\nmcv-85 mch-29.1 mchc-34.4 rdw-14.4 plt\\n 09:44pm blood neuts-44.0* lymphs-47.6* monos-5.1\\neos-2.6 baso-0.8\\n 09:44pm blood glucose-241* urean-27* creat-1.9* na-139\\nk-3.9 cl-101 hco3-28 angap-14\\n 09:48pm blood glucose-223* k-4.0\\n\\ndischarge:\\n 06:25am blood wbc-5.9 rbc-4.16* hgb-12.4 hct-35.6*\\nmcv-86 mch-29.8 mchc-34.8 rdw-14.2 plt\\n 06:25am blood glucose-183* urean-27* creat-1.3* na-138\\nk-4.0 cl-100 hco3-29 angap-13\\n 06:25am blood calcium-9.8 phos-4.1 mg-1.9\\n\\nimaging:\\nthe linear opacities noted at the right lung base have improved\\nto near resolution on the current study. no definite focal\\nconsolidation or superimposed edema noted.\\n\\nbrief hospital course:\\n with history of bipolar vs schizophrenia, diabetes mellitus mellitus, recent pna\\npresenting with chief complaint of not wanting to take her\\nlantus, found to be hyperglycemic with acute kidney injury.\\n\\nactive issues:\\n\\nhyperglycemia: secondary to self-reported insulin\\nnon-compliance. she says that her pcp recently switched her to\\nlong acting lantus rather than the humulin 70/30 that she had\\nused previously, and she did not feel that the lantus worked as\\nwell, so she stopped using it. fingerstick was 274 in the ed,\\nreached the 300s shortly after admission. no evidence of dka or\\nhyperosmolar state. she was treated with lantus + sliding scale\\ninsulin, however her sugars remained in the 200s due to diet\\nnon-compliance, i.e. she ate multiple ice creams, ices,\\nand crackers in the kitchen in addition to full meals.\\nbecause of her reluctance to take lantus, she was discharged on\\nher previous dose of 70/30 so that she would be getting some\\nform of insulin. her metformin had been held on admission, but\\nshe was advised to continue this at home. she was also given\\nnumerous materials on the components of a proper diabetic diet\\nand instructed to follow up with her pcp.\\n\\n : cr to 1.9 on admission from baseline of 1.0, likely\\npre-renal in setting of dehydration hyperglycemia.\\nlisinopril was held on admission. she was given fluids\\novernight and po hydration, and her cr improved to 1.3 the\\nfollowing morning. she was instructed to restart her lisinopril\\non discharge, however she was counseled on the importance of\\nkeeping her sugars better controlled and staying well-hydrated\\nto prevent acute renal injury again.\\n\\nchronic back pain: she was not given oxycodone during the\\nadmission, as her pcp has refused to prescribe this in the past.\\n he referred her to pain clinic, which she dnk'd. she threatened\\nto leave ama after we refused to give her oxycodone we had a\\nfrank conversation with her about this, and discussed that\\nnarcotic pain medications are often not the best treatment for\\nchronic pain. we again stressed the importance of being seen in\\nthe pain clinic. she was discharged with non-narcotic pain meds\\nfor her chronic back pain.\\n\\nabnormal u/a: initial ua suggested of possible infection,\\nhowever likely contaminated urine given epithelial cells. given\\n1 dose cipro overnight, ua was repeated and showed no sign of\\ninfection. not discharged with any antibiotics.\\n\\nchronic issues:\\n\\nschizophrenia/bipolar: stable, continued on her outpatient psych\\nmedications.\\n\\ntransfer of care issues:\\n1. diabetes mellitus mellitus: assess glycemic control on 70/30 regimen\\n\\nmedications on admission:\\n1. aripiprazole 15 mg tablet sig: one (1) tablet po daily\\n(daily).\\n2. clonazepam 1 mg tablet sig: two (2) tablet po qhs (once a day\\n\\n(at bedtime)).\\n3. clonidine 0.1 mg tablet sig: three (3) tablet po daily\\n(daily).\\n4. gabapentin 400 mg capsule sig: one (1) capsule po tid (3\\ntimes a day).\\n5. aspirin 81 mg tablet, chewable sig: one (1) tablet, chewable\\n\\npo daily (daily).\\n6. hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg capsule sig: one (1) capsule po\\ndaily (daily).\\n7. lisinopril 20 mg tablet sig: one (1) tablet po daily (daily).\\n\\n8. mirtazapine 15 mg tablet sig: three (3) tablet po hs (at\\nbedtime).\\n9. promethazine 25 mg tablet sig: two (2) tablet po daily\\n(daily).\\n10. oxycodone 5 mg tablet sig: one (1) tablet po q6-8hrs:prn\\npain as needed for pain for 5 days.\\ndisp:*10 tablet(s)* refills:*0*\\n11. guaifenesin 100 mg/5 ml syrup sig: mls po q6h (every 6\\n\\nhours) as needed for cough for 7 days.\\ndisp:*250 ml(s)* refills:*0*\\n12. nicotine 14 mg/24 hr patch 24 hr sig: one (1) patch 24 hr\\ntransdermal daily (daily) for 5 days.\\ndisp:*5 patch 24 hr(s)* refills:*0*\\n13. acetaminophen 500 mg tablet sig: two (2