mes a day as needed for pain.\\n14. insulin nph & regular human 100 unit/ml (70-30) suspension\\nsig: (45) units subcutaneous once a day: with\\nbreakfast.\\n15. humulin 70/30 100 unit/ml (70-30) suspension sig: ten (10)\\nunits subcutaneous with dinner.\\n16. albuterol sulfate 90 mcg/actuation hfa aerosol inhaler sig:\\none (1) puff inhalation every four (4) hours as needed for\\nshortness of breath or wheezing.\\n\\ndischarge disposition:\\nhome\\n\\ndischarge diagnosis:\\nprimary diagnosis:\\ndiabetes mellitus mellitus type ii\\nacute kidney injury\\n\\ndischarge condition:\\nmental status: clear and coherent.\\nlevel of consciousness: alert and interactive.\\nactivity status: ambulatory - independent.\\n\\ndischarge instructions:\\ndear ms. ,\\n\\nit was a pleasure to take care of you during your hospital stay\\nat . you were admitted to the hospital because of high\\nsugars from not taking your insulin and mild kidney damage from\\ndehydration. you improved after getting insulin and fluids.\\n\\nit is very, very important that you always remember to take your\\ninsulin as directed and try to stay away from sugary, fatty\\nfoods in order to keep your blood sugars down.\\n\\nchanges to your medications:\\nstart insulin 70/30 45 units in am, 10 units with dinner. you\\nwill need to check your fingersticks frequently to see if your\\nsugar drops.\\n\\nfollowup instructions:\",\n          \"name: unit no:\\n\\nadmission date: discharge date:\\n\\ndate of birth: sex: f\\n\\nservice: surgery\\n\\nallergies:\\ncodeine / oxycodone / ciprofloxacin\\n\\nattending: .\\n\\nchief complaint:\\nfever, chills\\n\\nmajor surgical or invasive procedure:\\nbedside incision and drainage\\n\\nhistory of present illness:\\nms. is a year old female s/p ventral hernia repair\\nwith mesh on . she tolerated this well but her postop\\ncourse was complicated by hospital acquired pneumonia for which\\nshe was given a 7 day course of levofloxacin to complete at\\nhome. she states that the levofloxacin caused nausea and\\nvomiting, so she only completed a 5 day course. her nausea\\nresolved after stopping her antibiotic. she was then in her\\nusual state until 4 days ago\\nwhen she started having subjective fevers, chills, fatigue, and\\nlack of appetite. she states her temperature during these days\\nwas primarily 100.4 which she medicated with tylenol. she comes\\nto the er today because of her persistent symptoms. she\\notherwise denies nausea, vomiting, following the period after\\nstopping her antibiotics. she has been passing flatus and having\\nnormal bowel movements. she has some abdominal pain which she\\nattributes to her incisions. she denies, weight loss, dizziness,\\nchest pain, shortness of breath, cough, dysuria, hematuria,\\nlower extremity edema, and new rashes.\\n\\npast medical history:\\nhtn\\ndiabetes mellitus mellitus-2\\ndiverticulitis\\nhyperlipidemia\\nthalassemia trait\\npneumonia\\nurinary incontinence\\nostopenia\\nfatty liver\\ncolonic adenoma\\nlichen planus\\nobesity\\nhordeolum\\n\\nsocial history:\\n\\nfamily history:\\nnon-contributory\\n\\nphysical exam:\\nvitals: temp 99.2, hr 69, bp 121/60, rr 18\\ngen:nad, aox3\\ncv:rrr\\nresp:ctab\\nabd: wound vac in place, suctioning well. when removed\\nappximately 3 inch length x1.5 inch width, 3inch depth well\\ngranulating wound on lower aspect of midline wound. staples\\nremoved. previous jp drain sites healing well without erythema\\next:no edema\\n\\npertinent results:\\nct abd/pelvis w/ contrast :\\n1. large rim-enhancing fluid collection with tiny locules of\\nair and adjacent stranding in the anterior subcutaneous tissues\\nis concerning for abscess.\\n2. smaller intra-abdominal rim-enhancing loculated fluid\\ncollection may\\nrepresent intra-abdominal abscess.\\n3. tiny focus of air in the bladder. correlate with history of\\ninstrumentation.\\n\\nbrief hospital course:\\nms. was admitted to the surgery floor on .\\nshe was started on iv antibiotics for her wound infection. a ct\\nabd/pel showed a subcutaneous abscess. on , she\\nunderwent bedside incision and drainage of her abscess.\\npurulent output was noted at this time. this was sent for\\nculture. her wound was packed with dressing. she underwent wound\\nvac placement on . her wound cultures retuned with mrsa\\nsensitive to bactrim. she was discharged home with a 2 week\\ncourse of bactrim. she was tolerating regular diet and her pain\\nwas controlled with tylenol at time of her discharge. she had no\\ncomplications or consultations during her hospital stay.\\n\\nmedications on admission:\\nniacin 1000 mg q day, atenolol 12.5 mg bid, metformin 1000mg\\nbid, aspirin 81 mg q day\\n\\ndischarge medications:\\n1. atenolol 25 mg tablet sig: 0.5 tablet po bid (2 times a day).\\n\\n2. acetaminophen 325 mg tablet sig: two (2) tablet po q6h (every\\n6 hours) as needed for fever and pain.\\n3. aspirin 81 mg tablet, chewable sig: one (1) tablet, chewable\\npo daily (daily).\\n4. metformin 500 mg tablet sig: one (1) tablet po bid (2 times a\\nday).\\n5. ferrous sulfate 300 mg (60 mg iron) tablet sig: one (1)\\ntablet po daily (daily).\\ndisp:*30 tablet(s)* refills:*2*\\n6. sulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim 800-160 mg tablet sig: one (1)\\ntablet po bid (2 times a day).\\ndisp:*28 tablet(s)* refills:*0*\\n\\ndischarge disposition:\\nhome with service\\n\\nfacility:\\n\\ndischarge diagnosis:\\nwound infection, wound abscess\\n\\ndischarge condition:\\nmental status: clear and coherent.\\nlevel of consciousness: alert and interactive.\\nactivity status: ambulatory - independent.\\n\\ndischarge instructions:\\ndear ms. ,\\nyou were admitted for management of a wound infection and\\nabscess collection along your recent hernia incision. your\\nincision was opened and wound cultures were sent to microbiology\\nwhich grew out a bacteria known as mrsa (methicillin resistant\\nstaph aureus). this organism can be treated with bactrim, which\\nyou currently are on. you will need to continue taking this\\nmedication for a short time after discharge.\\nyou had a wound vac placed on your abdomen which will need to be\\nin place for several weeks. a wound care nurse come to your\\nhouse to change put one on today. it will be changed again on\\n , and then again on at your appointment with\\n . ***please make sure to bring your wound vac supplies\\n(sponge and tape in packaging to your appointment with dr.\\non\\n\\nfollowup instructions:\",\n          \"name: unit no:\\n\\nadmission date: discharge date:\\n\\ndate of birth: sex: f\\n\\nservice: neurosurgery\\n\\nallergies:\\nsulfa (sulfonamide antibiotics) / morphine\\n\\nattending: .\\n\\nchief complaint:\\naneurysm coiling\\n\\nmajor surgical or invasive procedure:\\n - coiling of the acom aneurysm.\\n\\nhistory of present illness:\\n y/o female with incidental finding of a acom aneurysm who\\npresented today for elective coiling. the risks and benefits of\\nsurgical intervention were discussed and the patient consented\\nto the procedure.\\n\\npast medical history:\\nms\\n\\nsocial history:\\n\\nfamily history:\\nnc\\n\\nphysical exam:\\nphysical examination on discharge:\\nalert and oriented x3. speech fluent and clear. comprehension\\nintact.\\ncn ii-xii grossly intact.\\nmotor strength examination in the upper and lower\\nextremities bilaterally.\\ngroin site: + ecchymosis; soft and non-tender.\\n\\nbrief hospital course:\\nms. presented to the hospital to undergo an elective\\naneurysm coiling under general anesthesia. procedure was\\nuncomplicated, patient was extubated in the angio suite and\\nbrought to the icu for monitoing.\\n\\non , the patient remained stable and was transferred to the\\nfloor for one additional day of observation. overnight, she\\nexperienced one episode of hypotension with a sbp in the and\\na fall without head strike. her bp returned to normal limits\\nwithout intervention.\\n\\non , the patient remained neurologically stable. she was\\nambulating independently, tolerating a diet and voiding without\\ndifficulty. it was determined she would be discharged to home\\ntoday.\\n\\nmedications on admission:\\nthe preadmission medication list may be inaccurate and requires\\nfuther investigation.\\n1. naproxen 500 mg po q12h:prn pain.\\n\\ndischarge medications:\\n1. amlodipine 5 mg po daily\\n2. bupropion (sustained release) 100 mg po bid\\n3. citalopram 20 mg po daily\\n4. lisinopril 20 mg po bid\\n5. betaseron (interferon beta-1b) 0.3 mg subcutaneous every\\nother day\\n6. acetaminophen 325-650 mg po q6h:prn fever or pain\\ndo not exceed greater than 4g acetaminophen in a 24-hour period.\\n\\n7. oxycodone (immediate release) mg po q4h:prn pain\\nhold for sedation, drowsiness, or rr <12. do not drive while\\ntaking this medication.\\nrx *oxycodone 5 mg tablet(s) by mouth every 4 hours disp\\n#*30 tablet refills:*0\\n8. aspirin 325 mg po daily\\nrx *aspirin 325 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth daily disp #*30 tablet\\nrefills:*3\\n9. docusate sodium 100 mg po bid\\nrx *docusate sodium 100 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth twice a day disp\\n#*40 tablet refills:*0\\n10. hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg po daily\\n11. metformin (metformin) 500 mg po bid\\n\\ndischarge disposition:\\nhome\\n\\ndischarge diagnosis:\\nacom aneurysm\\n\\ndischarge condition:\\nmental status: clear and coherent.\\nlevel of consciousness: alert and interactive.\\nactivity status: ambulatory - independent.\\n\\ndischarge instructions:\\ndr.\\n\\nactivity\\n you may gradually return to your normal activities, but we\\nrecommend you take it easy for the next hours to avoid\\nbleeding from your groin.\\n heavy lifting, running, climbing, or other strenuous exercise\\nshould be avoided for ten (10) days. this is to prevent bleeding\\nfrom your groin.\\n you make take leisurely walks and slowly increase your\\nactivity at your own pace. try to do too much all at once.\\n do not go swimming or submerge yourself in water for five (5)\\ndays after your procedure.\\n you make take a shower.\\n\\nmedications\\