            "application/vnd.google.colaboratory.intrinsic+json": {
              "type": "dataframe",
              "variable_name": "preview",
              "summary": "{\n  \"name\": \"preview\",\n  \"rows\": 3,\n  \"fields\": [\n    {\n      \"column\": \"subject_id\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 3323281,\n        \"min\": 12165269,\n        \"max\": 18655830,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 3,\n        \"samples\": [\n          18655830,\n          12165269,\n          14170769\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"hadm_id\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"number\",\n        \"std\": 483656,\n        \"min\": 27912838,\n        \"max\": 28850231,\n        \"num_unique_values\": 3,\n        \"samples\": [\n          27912838,\n          28588276,\n          28850231\n        ],\n        \"semantic_type\": \"\",\n        \"description\": \"\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \"column\": \"clean_text\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"dtype\": \"string\",\n        \"num_unique_values\": 3,\n        \"samples\": [\n          \"name: unit no:\\n\\nadmission date: discharge date:\\n\\ndate of birth: sex: f\\n\\nservice: medicine\\n\\nallergies:\\nvancomycin / minoxidil / carvedilol\\n\\nattending: .\\n\\nchief complaint:\\nabd pain, nasuea, vomiting\\n\\nmajor surgical or invasive procedure:\\nnone\\n\\nhistory of present illness:\\nms. is a yo woman with a complex pmhx including\\ndm1, esrd on hd, cad s/p cabg in , gastroparesis, and c.\\ndiff presenting with nausea and abdominal pain. she was referred\\nfrom after her called saying that she is having abd\\npain and nausea. she has been taking compazine and suppositories\\nwithout effect. she began feeling overwhelmed and unable to cope\\nwith her symptoms and was advised to go to the ed.\\nplease see patient's last clinic note from for summary of\\nrecent history. in brief, she was hospitalized in for\\ncabg and had a course complicated by c difficile, now s/p\\ntreatment with flagyl and fidaxomicin. she also experienced\\nworsened nausea with morphine administration. she was admitted\\nagain in late for pain control and was\\nfound to have possible free air under the diaphragm; treated\\nwith bowel rest. currently she has been back at home and has\\ngradually advanced her diet to include chicken broth and some\\ntoast. per her clinic note on , her diarrhea and n/v had\\nimproved.\\non interview, patient stated that she has been having abdominal\\npain and vomiting for 1 week and it has gradually worsened. she\\nwas very anxious and tearful and had recently vomited despite\\nreceiving dilaudid and prochlorperazine. no blood in\\nstool/vomit, no fever/chills, no other symptoms. no chest pain\\nor difficulties with sternal incision. her diarrhea has overall\\nimproved since her c diff infection and she is now only having 1\\nbm per day. overall she states her symptoms are typical of her\\ngastroparesis flares but worse than usual.\\nshe performs home dialysis 5 times per week. her last dialysis\\nsession was last night. she makes little to no urine daily.\\nin the ed initial vitals were: 98.4 81\\n- labs were significant for lactate:1.6, k 3.1, cr 4.9, h/h\\n9.5/28.9\\n- physical exam was signficant for abdomen soft and tender to\\nmoderate palpation; tender in epigastrium but not lower\\nquadrants. sternal wound healing well. no edema. per nursing,\\nstool soft but not diarrhea.\\n- patient was given labetalol 5mg iv x2, 1.5 mg iv dilaudid, 500\\ncc ns, 5u lispro, 4mg zofran, 5mg prochlorperazine\\n- cxr showed no free intraperitoneal air or acute\\ncardiopulmonary process.\\n\\npast medical history:\\nanemia of chronic disease\\nanexiety\\nbulimia, resolved\\ndepression\\ndiabetes mellitus mellitus, insulin dependent\\ndiabetic ketoacidosis\\ndiabetic neuropathy\\ndiabetic retinopathy\\nend-stage renal disease on hd\\ngastroparesis\\nhypertension\\nhypothyroidism\\nosteopenia\\novarian cyst\\npericarditis\\nperitonitis\\nstress fracture of metatarsal\\nvertebral fracture\\n\\nsocial history:\\n\\nfamily history:\\ngrandfather (paternal) - died of myocardial infarction in his\\nearly .\\nmother - died of ovarian cancer\\nfather - history of hypertension\\n\\nphysical exam:\\nadmission physical exam:\\nvitals - 98.4, 166/84, 74, 99% on 2l\\ngeneral: tearful, uncomfortable\\nheent: at/nc, eomi, perrl, anicteric sclera, pink conjunctiva,\\ndry mm\\nneck: nontender supple neck,\\ncardiac: iii/vi sem, rrr, well healing sternotomy scar\\nlung: ctab, no wheezes, rales, rhonchi, breathing comfortably\\nwithout use of accessory muscles\\nabdomen: nondistended, +bs, ttp in epigastrum, no\\nrebound/guarding,\\nextremities: no cyanosis, clubbing or edema, moving all 4\\nextremities with purpose\\npulses: 2+ dp pulses bilaterally\\nskin: warm and well perfused, few tattoos\\ndischarge physical exam:\\nvs: t 978 146/72 (systolin 144-180) hr 71 (58-71) 98 ra\\ngeneral: comfortable appearing, sleeping comfortably in bed\\nheent: anicteric sclera, pink conjunctiva, mmm\\nneck: nontender and supple\\ncardiac: ii/vi sem, rrr, well healing sternotomy scar\\nlung: ctab, no wheezes, rales, rhonchi, breathing comfortably\\nabdomen: nondistended, +bs, nontender to palpation, no\\nrebound/guarding, no hsm\\nextremities: no cyanosis, clubbing or edema, moving all 4\\nextremities equally\\npulses: 2+ dp pulses bilaterally\\nskin: no rash, dry\\n\\npertinent results:\\ninitial labs:\\n\\n 01:48pm plt count-216\\n 01:48pm neuts-73.2* monos-4.4 eos-1.2\\nbasos-0.6\\n 01:48pm wbc-5.6 rbc-3.25* hgb-9.5* hct-28.9* mcv-89\\nmch-29.2 mchc-32.8 rdw-18.5*\\n 01:48pm hcg-7\\n 01:48pm albumin-4.2\\n 01:48pm lipase-31\\n 01:48pm alt(sgpt)-28 ast(sgot)-37 alk phos-93 tot\\nbili-0.4\\n 01:48pm estgfr-using this\\n 01:48pm glucose-241* urea n-13 creat-4.9*# sodium-140\\npotassium-3.1* chloride-96 total co2-26 anion gap-21*\\n 01:58pm lactate-1.6\\n 01:58pm comments-green top\\ndischarge labs:\\n\\n 06:13am blood wbc-7.1 rbc-2.91* hgb-8.7* hct-26.3*\\nmcv-90 mch-29.7 mchc-32.9 rdw-19.2* plt\\n 06:10am blood wbc-5.0 rbc-2.94* hgb-8.7* hct-26.5*\\nmcv-90 mch-29.6 mchc-32.9 rdw-18.6* plt\\n 06:13am blood plt\\n 06:10am blood plt\\n 06:13am blood glucose-190* urean-18 creat-6.0*# na-140\\nk-3.7 cl-102 hco3-28 angap-14\\n 06:10am blood glucose-168* urean-11 creat-4.2*# na-136\\nk-3.8 cl-97 hco3-29 angap-14\\n 06:13am blood calcium-8.8 phos-5.8*# mg-2.1\\n 06:10am blood calcium-8.6 phos-2.8# mg-2.0\\n\\nreports:\\n ecg sinus rhythm. poor r wave progression, probably\\nnormal variant. diffuse repolarization abnormalities that are\\nnon-specific. compared to the previous tracing of\\nmultiple described abnormalities persist without significant\\nchange. clinical correlation is suggested.\\n\\n ap/lateral:\\nfindings: prior enteric tube is not visualized. the lungs are\\nnow clear, prior effusions have essentially resolved. there is\\nno consolidation or pulmonary edema. cardiac silhouette is\\nmildly enlarged. median sternotomy wires are again noted. no\\nacute osseous abnormalities. no free intraperitoneal air.\\nimpression: no free intraperitoneal air or acute cardiopulmonary\\nprocess.\\n\\nbrief hospital course:\\n with type 1 diabetes c/b nephropathy, esrd on hd, cad s/p cabg in\\n , recent c diff infection and gastroparesis who presented\\nwith nausea and abdominal pain c/f gastroparesis flare thought\\nto be multifactorial related to gastroparesis, sensitivity to\\nuremia and depression anxiety. patient much improved on\\ndischarge after treatment with antinausea, antipain and anti\\nanxiety medications and dialysis. at discharge she was\\ntolerating po.\\n\\nconsults: gi\\n\\n# nausea/vomiting/abdominal pain- patient presented with nausea\\nand vomiting consistent with prior gastroparesis flares. nausea\\nresolved initially after dialysis but then worsened. she is\\nthought to be very sensitive to uremia (despite the fact that\\nbun on admission was 26) she was treated with iv pain\\nantiemetics and antianxiety and on discharge nausea had resolved\\nand patient was tolerating po. patient is tolerating po food\\nand medications. likely has large psych component with\\ndepression and anxiety contributing. patient was discharged with\\npo zofran odt and pr compazine (patient reports that she has\\nboth meds at home already) she was also discharged with short\\ncourse of po lorazepam (0.5mg bid:prn) she will f/u with pcp\\n and will schedule an appointment with dr. .\\n\\n# hypertension patient was htn on admission as not tolerating po\\nmedications, this improved with meds and hd and on her home meds\\n hydralazine and metoprolol tartrate. she was persistently\\nhypertensive to 180s and required getting her meds early several\\ntimes. bp meds should be titrated as an outpatient.\\n\\n#cad s/p recent cabg pt with recent cabg in . sternotomy\\nscar is healing well. no anginal symptoms and no new findings on\\nekg on admission. she was continued on asa, metoprolol\\n\\n# dmi: poorly controlled type 1 diabetes mellitus in past, complicated by\\ngastroparesis and end stage renal disease. followed by\\n(dr. . patient reduced glargine from 10u bid to 5u bid\\nas had been not eating. patient discharged on glargine 5 units\\nbreakfast glargine 5 units bedtime but home insulin should be\\ntitrated as she tolerates more food at home.\\n\\n# esrd: she performs her own hd at home through a lue fistula.\\nshe had dialysis . followed by dr. at . she\\nwill resume home hd on discharge.\\n\\n# hypothyroidism: continue home levothyroxine\\n\\ntransitional:\\n-please advise patient if not eating she should decrease lantus\\nto 5mg bid\\n-patient discharged on new medications lorazepam and\\npantoprazole.\\n-bps were elevated to systolic 190 on this admission patient may\\nrequire uptitration of bp meds as an outpatient.\\n- missed recent surgical appointment, patient will call on\\n to reschedule this\\n# code: full\\n# emergency contact: brother\\n\\nmedications on admission:\\nthe preadmission medication list is accurate and complete.\\n1. fidaxomicin 200 mg oral bid\\n2. aspirin ec 81 mg po daily\\n3. atorvastatin 80 mg po qpm\\n4. hydralazine 10 mg po q8h\\n5. levothyroxine sodium 100 mcg po daily\\n6. metoprolol tartrate 25 mg po tid\\n7. glargine 5 units breakfast\\nglargine 5 units bedtime\\ninsulin sc sliding scale using novolog insulin\\n\\ndischarge medications:\\n1. aspirin ec 81 mg po daily\\n2. atorvastatin 80 mg po qpm\\n3. hydralazine 10 mg po q8h\\n4. glargine 5 units breakfast\\nglargine 5 units bedtime\\ninsulin sc sliding scale using novolog insulin\\n5. levothyroxine sodium 100 mcg po daily\\n6. metoprolol tartrate 25 mg po tid\\n7. fidaxomicin 200 mg oral bid\\n8. lorazepam 0.5 mg po bid\\nrx *lorazepam 0.5 mg 1 tablet by mouth twice a day disp #*14\\ntablet refills:*0\\n9. pantoprazole 40 mg po q24h\\nrx *pantoprazole 40 mg 1 tablet(s) by mouth daily disp #*28\\ntablet refills:*0\\n\\ndischarge disposition:\\nhome with service\\n\\nfacility:\\n\\ndischarge diagnosis:\\nfinal diagnosis:\\ngastroparesis\\nesrd on hd\\n\\nsecondary diagnosis:\\ncad s/p cabg\\ndiabetes mellitus type i\\n\\ndischarge condition:\\nmental status: clear and coherent.\\nlevel of consciousness: alert and interactive.\\nactivity status: ambulatory - independent.\\n\\ndischarge instructions:\\ndear ms. ,\\n\\nyou were admitted to the hospital with abdominal pain and\\nnausea. this was most likely from the gastroparesis you had\\nbefore. you were treated with medicatiosn to treat your pain,\\nnausea, and anxietyn and improved quickly. the\\ngastroenterologists saw you in the hospital and recommended an\\nacid inhibitor called pantoprazole. you also had dialysis on\\n during this admission.\\n\\nit is great to see you doing better. please continue to take\\nall your medications and see your doctors for follow up after\\ndischarge.\\n\\n-your care team-\\n\\nfollowup instructions:\",\n          \"name: unit no:\\n\\nadmission date: discharge date:\\n\\ndate of birth: sex: f\\n\\nservice: medicine\\n\\nallergies:\\namoxicillin / shellfish\\n\\nattending: .\\n\\nchief complaint:\\nchest pain\\n\\nmajor surgical or invasive procedure:\\nnone\\n\\nhistory of present illness:\\nms. is an woman with pmh of hfref (ef 42%),\\nas\\ns/p tavr ( ) c/b periprocedural cardiac arrest, cad s/p\\ncabg, esrd on hd (mwf) who presents from outpatient dialysis\\nwith\\nchest pain and shortness of breath.\\n\\nshe reports that the chest pain began during dialysis about 3.5\\nhours into treatment and was accompanied by shortness of breath,\\nboth of which resolved on arrival to the ed. she denies chest\\npain with exertion or at rest. this is the second time she has\\nhad chest pain at dialysis brought on after 3+ hours, and never\\nhad symptoms when treatment lasted under 3. she continues to\\nhave\\na mild cough with associated shortness of breath.\\n\\nshe denies fever/chills, v/d, abdominal pain.\\n\\nin the ed initial vitals were: 98.2 127/27 19 98%ra\\nlabs/studies notable for:\\n139 99 7 agap=13\\n-------------< 104\\n4.2 27 1.3\\n- phos 1.2\\n- 4.8 > < 151\\n- bnp 33940\\n- inr 1.1\\n- trop 0.52, mb 5 @\\n- 7.45/44\\n- lactate 1.8\\n\\ncxr: new, mild pulmonary edema with unchanged bilateral pleural\\neffusions, atelectasis, and cardiomegaly.\\n\\npatient was given:\\niv furosemide 120 mg\\npo aspirin 243 mg\\nneutra-phos 1 pkt\\nclopidogrel 75 mg\\npantoprazole 40 mg\\nlevothyroxine sodium 50 mcg\\nmetoprolol succinate xl 50 mg\\nmontelukast 10 mg\\nferrous gluconate 324 mg\\nisosorbide mononitrate (extended release) 120 mg\\niv furosemide 120 mg\\nfluticasone propionate nasal 2 spry\\nfluticasone-salmeterol diskus (250/50) 1 inh\\n\\nremained stable throughout the day, without further chest pain.\\nadmitted to for further workup.\\n\\nvitals on transfer: afebrile, 119/60, 81, 100% on 3l\\n\\non the floor, patient was chest pain free, denying shortness of\\nbreath. she has not noticed increased leg swelling, or\\ndyspnea/chest pain outside the setting of dialysis.\\n\\npast medical history:\\n1. cardiac risk factors\\n- type 2 diabetes mellitus mellitus with nephropathy and retinopathy\\n- hypertension\\n- hyperlipidemia\\n2. cardiac history\\n- cabg\\n- tavr\\n- congestive heart failure, diastolic dysfunction\\n3. other past medical history\\n- chronic renal disease\\n- chronic anemia likely secondary to chronic renal insufficiency\\n- iron deficiency anemia\\n- asthma (fvc 66%, fev1 76%)\\n- diverticulosis\\n- cartotid stenosis s/p cea\\n- tia\\n- gout\\n- temporal arteritis\\n\\nsocial history:\\n\\nfamily history:\\n- mother had mi in her\\n- grandmother had diabetes mellitus\\n\\nphysical exam:\\nadmission exam\\nvitals: afebrile, 119/60, 81, 100% on 3l\\ngeneral: well-developed, well-nourished. nad. mood, affect\\nappropriate.\\nheent: ncat. sclera anicteric. perrl, eomi. conjunctiva pink, no\\npallor or cyanosis of the oral mucosa. no xanthelasma.\\nneck: supple. no jvd.\\ncardiac: rrr, normal s1, s2. ii/vi late systolic murmur, loudest\\nat the left upper sternal border with radiation to the left\\ncarotid. no thrills, lifts.\\nlungs: no chest wall deformities. resp were unlabored, no\\naccessory muscle use. no crackles, wheezes or rhonchi.\\nabdomen: soft, ntnd. no hsm or tenderness.\\nextremities: trace peripheral edema.\\nskin: no stasis dermatitis, ulcers, scars, or xanthomas.\\npulses: distal pulses palpable and symmetric.\\n\\ndischarge exam\\nvitals: 97.7-98.5 61-66 93-100% 1l\\ngen: comfortable, in nad\\nneck: supple, jvp 8 cm\\nlungs: decreased breath sounds at lung bases. no wheezes or\\nrales\\ncardiac: regular rate and rhythm. harsh systolic murmur at usb.\\n\\nextremities: no lower extremity edema\\n\\npertinent results:\\nadmission labs\\n 09:10pm blood wbc-4.8 rbc-2.65* hgb-8.1* hct-25.0*\\nmcv-94 mch-30.6 mchc-32.4 rdw-18.0* rdwsd-58.6* plt\\n 09:10pm blood ptt-27.4\\n 09:10pm blood glucose-104* urean-7 creat-1.3* na-139\\nk-4.2 cl-99 hco3-27 angap-13\\n 09:10pm blood ck-mb-5\\n 09:10pm blood ctropnt-0.52*\\n 09:10pm blood calcium-8.4 phos-1.2* mg-1.8\\n 09:18pm blood po2-19* pco2-44 ph-7.45\\ncaltco2-32* base xs-4\\n\\ninterval labs\\n 05:55am blood ck-mb-4 ctropnt-0.55*\\n 12:40pm blood ctropnt-0.49*\\n\\ndischarge labs\\n 06:24am blood wbc-4.4 rbc-2.89* hgb-8.8* hct-27.1*\\nmcv-94 mch-30.4 mchc-32.5 rdw-18.0* rdwsd-59.3* plt\\n 06:24am blood glucose-109* urean-13 creat-2.0* na-141\\nk-3.6 cl-98 hco3-30 angap-13\\n\\nmicrobiology\\nblood culture : pnd\\n\\n 1:29 pm swab source: from hd catheter site.\\ngram stain (final :\\n 1+ (<1 per 1000x field): polymorphonuclear\\nleukocytes.\\n no microorganisms seen.\\nwound culture (preliminary): no growth.\\n\\nblood culture :\\n\\nimaging\\n\\necho\\n\\nthe left atrial volume index is severely increased. no atrial\\nseptal defect is seen by 2d or color doppler. the estimated\\nright atrial pressure is mmhg. left ventricular wall\\nthicknesses and cavity size are normal. there is mild global\\nleft ventricular hypokinesis (quantitative (biplane) lvef = 41\\n%). the right ventricular cavity is mildly dilated with moderate\\nglobal free wall hypokinesis. [intrinsic right ventricular\\nsystolic function is likely more depressed given the severity of\\ntricuspid regurgitation.] there is abnormal septal\\nmotion/position consistent with right ventricular\\npressure/volume overload. a 3 aortic valve bioprosthesis\\nis present. the aortic valve prosthesis appears well seated,\\nwith normal leaflet/disc motion and transvalvular gradients.\\ntrace aortic regurgitation is seen. the mitral valve leaflets\\nare mildly thickened. there is no mitral valve prolapse.\\nmoderate (2+) mitral regurgitation is seen. [due to acoustic\\nshadowing, the severity of mitral regurgitation may be\\nsignificantly underestimated.] severe [4+] tricuspid\\nregurgitation is seen. there is moderate pulmonary artery\\nsystolic hypertension. [in the setting of at least moderate to\\nsevere tricuspid regurgitation, the estimated pulmonary artery\\nsystolic pressure may be underestimated due to a very high right\\natrial pressure.] there is no pericardial effusion.\\n\\nimpression: bilateral pleural effusions are present. well seated\\n 3 tavr in aortic position with normal gradients/leaflet\\nmotion and no paravalvular leak. normal left ventricular wall\\nthiciness with normal cavity size, and moderate global\\nhypokinesis. restrictive filling pattern. at least moderate\\nmitral rgurgitation. severe tricuspid regurgitation. at least\\nmoderate pulmonary artery systolic hypertension. evidence of\\npressure/volume overload.\\n\\ncompared with the prior study (images reviewed) of ,\\nthe degree of mitral regurgitation appears slightly less. other\\nfindings appear similar.\\n\\ncxr\\nnew, mild pulmonary edema with unchanged bilateral pleural\\neffusions,\\natelectasis, and cardiomegaly.\\n\\nbrief hospital course:\\nms. is a female with history of hfref (ef\\n42%), as s/p tavr ( ) complicated by\\ncardiac arrest, cad status post cabg, esrd on hd (mwf) who\\npresents from clinic with chest pain or shortness of\\nbreath, likely related demand ischemia in setting of dialysis.\\n\\nacute issues\\n==============\\n#chest pain\\n#cad - patient initially presented with chest pain and shortness\\nof breath in the setting of dialysis, brought on after 3+ hours\\nof dialysis. of note patient had recent hospitalization\\n( ) for similar complaints. ekg was similar to prior\\nwithout ischemic changes. troponin peaked at 0.55, however\\nbaseline troponin is approximately 0.5, likely in the setting of\\nesrd and hfref. shortness of breath and chest pain were likely\\nrelated to demand ischemia in the setting of dialysis.\\nanti-anginals were up-titrated as part of medication\\noptimization. home metoprolol xl was increased from 50 mg to 75\\nmg bid. in addition home imdur 120mg po qd was initially reduced\\nand fractionated to isordil 30 mg tid. she was continued on\\naspirin 81 mg po qd, plavix 75 mg po qd, atorvastatin 80 mg po\\nqd, amlodipine 2.5 mg po qhs, and slng prn. patient remained\\nchest pain-free without dyspnea during hemodialysis sessions\\nduring hospitalization. per renal, will discharge patient on\\nincreased dose of home torsemide 40mg bid on non-dialysis days\\nand volume management per hd on hd days (mwf). discharge weight:\\n51.94 kg.\\n\\n#anemia - patient initially presented with hemoglobin of 6.8\\nfrom a baseline of . she is status post 1 unit prbc with\\npost-transfusion hemoglobin 9.5 after dialysis. there was no\\nobvious source of bleeding and she denied bloody stools or\\nhematuria. stool guaiac was negative. iron studies were\\nconsistent with anemia of chronic disease. there was no evidence\\nof hemolysis. patient has a history of chronic anemia and was\\ncontinued on home ferrous sulfate 325 mg po daily.\\n\\n#tunneled line erythema - patient was noted to have erythema and\\nmild tenderness to palpation around right chest tunneled line\\ninsertion site, with serous drainage. swab cultures showed 1+\\npolys with no growth to date. blood cultures were also pending\\nat time of discharge. patient remained afebrile without\\nleukocytosis.\\n\\n#hfref (ef 42%) - per ed report, she appeared volume overloaded\\non arrival with bnp >33,000 chest x-ray showing new mild\\npulmonary edema. patient is status post iv lasix 120 mg in the\\ned with significant improvement on arrival to the floor.\\ntorsemide 20 mg po bid was held on admission, with volume\\nmanagement per hd. she was discharged on torsemide 40bid on\\nnon-dialysis days.\\n\\nchronic issues\\n==============\\n#htn - continued on amlodipine 2.5 mg po qhs and metoprolol 75\\nxl bid per above\\n#s/p tavr - continued home plavix 75 mg po qd\\n#diabetes mellitus mellitus - on lantus 5u qhs\\n#hypothyroidism - continued on home levothyroxine 50mcg\\nand 100mcg\\n#copd - continued on mometasone-formoterol, montelukast, and\\nalbuterol inhalers\\n#gerd - continued home pantoprazole 40 mg po q24h\\n#galucoma - continue